select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "ed06cc37-b3fc-4335-aa7e-354cc47b78d6", "episode_id": "b8d76cb1-de4e-4e7d-b3a5-39a3ce5e1f3b", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4872", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Colton Jacobs (39:37): Yeah, I think so. I haven't found it. I mean, I've seen examples of it. I've seen people, photographers that I'm like, whoa, this is, I'll find a photographer that I really look up to, or I see his incredible work and I see 'em all over the place for some big brand, like tons of work. And then you go to their portfolio, like their Instagram or their website, and it's like this stuff's all old and looks like it hasn't really been managed. They're not. And I think once you build that relationship with enough industry people in your industry and you have developed a reputation, word of mouth, and just the sheer amount of people that you have worked with and they had a good taste of working with you is enough because you've got this network of people that liked you and liked working with you, that they don't have to go look at your website every time they think of you because they know, oh, last time I worked with 'em, he was amazing. (40:45): And so those are the type, oftentimes you'll go find that person on Instagram. Like me, I'm one of the millennials who go and look at everyone on Instagram. I hear a name like, oh, that photographer's awesome, even though I saw him in a magazine or something. I go look 'em up on Instagram. And then there's no photos. They don't really share. There's photos of their kids and vacations. You're like, is this the right one? And then you look at tagged photos and then you see all of their commercial. They pay no attention to their Instagram, but brands will tag 'em occasionally, and you're like, so I have seen that on display of those guys. Those people have made it. And I think that will come, but it does not come easily years in the industry. I would venture to say 10 plus 15, 10 or 15 years in the industry, not 10 plus 15, 10 to 15 years of solid time in the industry before you've built those relationships and networked with literally hundreds, maybe thousands of people.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:46): That also sounds kind of discouraging.", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["Colton Jacobs", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:37", "end_timestamp": "41:46", "podcast_index": 74, "episode_name": "EP 74: Colton Jacobs | Finding Work as a Photographer | Colton Jacobs Photography", "slug": "ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "guest_name": "Colton Jacobs", "guest_title": "Photographer", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Colton Jacobs, a Seattle-based adventure photographer shares his thoughts on finding and keeping clients. Colton has shot with many brands such as Filson, K2, Hest, and many other reputable brands.", "podcast_length": "44:96"}, {"id": "d8f4687f-d4be-45a8-b9b2-3adc207960ea", "episode_id": "b8d76cb1-de4e-4e7d-b3a5-39a3ce5e1f3b", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4872", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Colton Jacobs (41:48): Yeah. Well, I talked to a few when I was, was 18 or 19, and I was wanting to do this, and I had friends that were elite photographers in Seattle. They weren't outdoor sports or advertising photographers, but they straight up, it is a long game to find success as a freelance photographer. There's so much to learn. There's so many mistakes to be made, I think. Yeah, and it can be discouraging to know that. I would say you have to spin. There's going to be, I don't want to say this for sure, because a lot of people that are really, really good at the business side of things and learn quick. But I mean for me, I don't know, five years I'd say is like before you start figuring out that you think you know what you're doing going into each shoot, you kind of feel confident that you can figure out whatever happens. But I think if you can assist under someone and learn from them, I learned in a studio, I assisted at a big studio in Seattle and learned a lot of lighting technique and studio product photography, and that was awesome. And you learn a lot of stuff there. So if you can be an assistant or an intern, that's a good way to learn quick.\n\nCole Heilborn (43:27): What keeps you going? Why do you keep showing up,", "token_estimate": 313, "speakers": ["Colton Jacobs", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "41:48", "end_timestamp": "43:27", "podcast_index": 74, "episode_name": "EP 74: Colton Jacobs | Finding Work as a Photographer | Colton Jacobs Photography", "slug": "ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "guest_name": "Colton Jacobs", "guest_title": "Photographer", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Colton Jacobs, a Seattle-based adventure photographer shares his thoughts on finding and keeping clients. Colton has shot with many brands such as Filson, K2, Hest, and many other reputable brands.", "podcast_length": "44:96"}, {"id": "445f4515-03a5-4341-b2ec-e4a87e208480", "episode_id": "b8d76cb1-de4e-4e7d-b3a5-39a3ce5e1f3b", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4872", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Colton Jacobs (43:33): Man, I love the thrill of looking for that next great composition and capturing it, taking some idea in your head and if you can, the greatest is when you can take an idea, pitch it to a brand, they buy in, and then you go create it. That's awesome. That's awesome. I mean, the taking a concept, whether it's in a studio and you're manipulating light or it's, or it's on the field and you had to, you're suffering to get to a spot. You're on top of a mountain at sunrise and your fingers are freezing cold and the sun finally lines up and you get the right shot with the athlete or whatever it is, wherever you're at, and whatever the medium is, the art of creating that in photo or video, that short snapshot of capturing that, especially when it was premeditated, a plan that came to fruition is so satisfying. I mean, or it wasn't premeditated. Maybe you just, I mean, you love setting yourself up for that experience. I mean, I wish I could find a way to pay, make sunset's pay or sunrise's pay, because standing on the top of a peak in the north cascades at sunset or sunrise is by far my favorite place in the world. But setting yourself up, there's probably a way. I mean, there's ways to make anything pay. (45:19): The satisfaction is what keeps me going of creating those moments, capturing those shots. And for me, it's usually photographing a athlete or an individual participating in a sport that I, for one love and creating that. I mean, it's creating that marketable material for a brand that tells a story of this is why that keeps me going, because I for one, want to buy that product because this is an image I'm proud of that I went out and created, motivates people to want to go participate in that. And so that's kind of like the field that I feel like I've really focused my attention on, and I get a lot of satisfaction out of creating those moments.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:14): Yeah. Well said. We're running out of time for today, but if people want to pick up this conversation and bug you for any continued thoughts about finding work and just kind of surviving in the photo world, where can people find you?", "token_estimate": 539, "speakers": ["Colton Jacobs", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "43:33", "end_timestamp": "46:14", "podcast_index": 74, "episode_name": "EP 74: Colton Jacobs | Finding Work as a Photographer | Colton Jacobs Photography", "slug": "ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "guest_name": "Colton Jacobs", "guest_title": "Photographer", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Colton Jacobs, a Seattle-based adventure photographer shares his thoughts on finding and keeping clients. Colton has shot with many brands such as Filson, K2, Hest, and many other reputable brands.", "podcast_length": "44:96"}, {"id": "e380d29e-3ced-4bc7-ab62-9772d2fea7b9", "episode_id": "b8d76cb1-de4e-4e7d-b3a5-39a3ce5e1f3b", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4872", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Colton Jacobs (46:34): They can find me on the internet, colton jacobs.com or Instagram, Colton Andrew Jacobs dot or just Colton? Andrew Jacobs, yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:46): Right on. Cool, man. Well, it's been great getting to chat. This is definitely a topic that could continue on for many hours, but I highly recommend that people go check out your work, Colton. It's pretty phenomenal, and I am excited to see what you create in the future.\n\nColton Jacobs (47:03): Thank you. It's been a pleasure chatting with you. And yes, please ask me any questions if you guys have any. Maybe I'll have an answer.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:13): Maybe he won't though. That's the beauty of it all. Who knows? We never really know what we know.\n\nColton Jacobs (47:19): We never know. Don't be afraid to fail.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:23): Have a good rest of your day.\n\nColton Jacobs (47:24): Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:25): See you. Thank you for listening to this of the Backcountry Marketing podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 280, "speakers": ["Colton Jacobs", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "46:34", "end_timestamp": "47:25", "podcast_index": 74, "episode_name": "EP 74: Colton Jacobs | Finding Work as a Photographer | Colton Jacobs Photography", "slug": "ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-74-colton-jacobs-finding-work-as-a-photographer-colton-jacobs-photography", "guest_name": "Colton Jacobs", "guest_title": "Photographer", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Colton Jacobs, a Seattle-based adventure photographer shares his thoughts on finding and keeping clients. Colton has shot with many brands such as Filson, K2, Hest, and many other reputable brands.", "podcast_length": "44:96"}, {"id": "20df9651-51a3-4f9f-87df-41ff5142fb3a", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Evan Poitras (00:05): You need to be telling the narrative. The boy who cried wolf is don't lie, but it's told through a story that you understand why you shouldn't. And so that's what content needs to be, in my opinion, and less literal. And I think that's where the endlessness of what we talked about is paramount because there's so much that with Viking still, there's so much room left to tell narratives that have a subtle underlying premise to take away.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:31): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole, he Warren, and today I'm sitting down with Evan Pores. He's the content marketing manager for Norco. Evan, how are you doing?\n\nEvan Poitras (01:12): I'm doing great, Cole. How are you\n\nCole Heilborn (01:14): Doing? Fantastic. Yeah, super excited to have you on the show today.\n\nEvan Poitras (01:18): Yeah, super excited to be here. I've been listening to some past episodes and they're all very entertaining, so hopefully I can keep the level high.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:26): And some of the recent episodes have been pretty close to home for you. You're up in Whistler, right?\n\nEvan Poitras (01:31): I'm actually in Vancouver now. I used to be up in Whistler, but now I live in beautiful, sunny Vancouver, bc. I lived in Whistler for about five years before this. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:41): Okay, awesome. Awesome. Well, before we jump in, I'm curious, besides biking, I'm assuming you're into biking, what type of hobbies or activities do you typically find yourself doing?", "token_estimate": 524, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:05", "end_timestamp": "01:41", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "20ced3b1-5328-433f-bfe9-794aa79f7580", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Evan Poitras (01:54): Yeah, biking is the obvious one. I can see five bikes while we're on this call right now, so that's in my eyesight. But yeah, other than biking, a lot of snowboarding in the winter up in Whistler and around the area of BC is kind of my main winter activity. Lots of camping, road tripping, some hiking, kind of the bits and pieces of everything outside, but mostly dominated by biking. Biking kind of dictates most of my fun calendar through the year, so\n\nCole Heilborn (02:31): That's the pace\n\nEvan Poitras (02:33): Even especially being down in Vancouver, the biking season is quite long compared to most other places that are mountainous, so it's definitely my main one. But yeah, try to get a bit of everything in every year.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:44): Cool. Well, I'm excited to dig into our topic today. As we mentioned, you're the content marketing manager for Norco. Could you kind of paint us a little bit of a background of what your job entails?\n\nEvan Poitras (02:55): Yeah, so my job first of all was brand new when I stepped into it, which was quite interesting, but it's kind of formed over the past two and a half years into, I'm the person who sort of sets the plans and the points and the things we're going to hit each year, timing both timing and subject. So if we've got bikes to launch, subjects easy, but when we don't, what are we talking about? What themes are we trying to hit? When are we doing them? What times are we going to lighten up the content? Are we going to be pushing heavier, harder campaigns? And then seeing those through from briefing our creative team all the way through to the social side and the details of launch day and everything there. So kind of play it both 30,000 feet and in the weeds and kind of balance the day-to-day ongoings of the industry and what's going on with all our athletes with trying to plan out long-term plans for a variety of campaigns.", "token_estimate": 482, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:54", "end_timestamp": "02:55", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "c8b68382-5383-4542-bb3e-c19be29ecc8a", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:58): Gotcha. Right on. So we're talking about content today. I think everyone talks about content. We all know what content, that content is important. I'm hoping we can kind of get beyond some of those more simple or not simple, but those topics we've all heard a lot about. But before we jump in, let's define content. How Norco do you define content?\n\nEvan Poitras (04:19): To me, content is anything you put out in it. Whether you're buying a digital ad that's 400 by 400, whatever's in that box is content or you're writing. I mean, we went through a rebrand process and our copywriter wrote a brand book working with our creative director. That's content and everything in between. Any sort of information that a brand is trying to push to somebody in my opinion is a form of content. I'm more involved in some than others obviously, but I think anything you're trying to tell a consumer is at this point content.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:55): Some of the obvious forms of content I think we think of are Instagram photos, videos, that type of stuff. But as you're saying this, I'm realizing what maybe even things like an email signature or website copy on the bottom footer of the website, that's all considered content.\n\nEvan Poitras (05:13): Yeah, I think so. I think anything, like I said, you're putting out there, I think you do tend to think more towards, especially in the bike industry, there's a lot of video component, photos, captions, but when you are working with people, I mean it's the footer of your email you send consumers has to be in line and things like that. So while the content is the attraction, maybe the executions more of the detail side that you want to be lined up, but any story or anything, any kind of information you're passing on to people, I would say content,", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "03:58", "end_timestamp": "05:13", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "a5b0ac40-3cbb-4b02-bfaa-669fc90a945a", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:48): What I appreciate about it, our call, gosh, whenever we connected last time was we were talking about this idea of content and obviously content is important. I think we could probably rattle off some stats that suggest that. But what you brought up and I thought was really interesting was this idea that managing that content and making sure that it's cohesive and that it's organized and that it supports the brand. And when you're dealing with, I don't know, lots of content that's got to get really complicated really quick. So could you elaborate a little bit on the problem or the challenge that you see?\n\nEvan Poitras (06:28): Yeah, so I think the challenge with it is in this world of endless content, God, how many times are we going to say content on this one? But you don't know if someone has seen one thing that you've produced or they're seeing everything that you produce. And so where that becomes a challenge is as a brand, you should always be trying to hit your brand pillars or however you're going to phrase the themes of your brand. You want to communicate with the consumer. So if they see one thing and you miss the mark there, you've missed them. But if they see everything and you're kind of wandering in how you're executing, they're getting confused as to what you are. So it's kind of a catch of you've got to nail it the first time, but you've got to keep nailing it. You can't let off the gas pedal at that point and either let them down or change how they perceive you. So it is a challenge trying to execute, and there's so many little details about how you take photos and how you show for the most part in the visual space and in the word space, how you're communicating and the language you're using to both hit those points and stay consistent while creating content piece after piece after piece. Just in this world, we're all making so much. So it is a challenge and it's a challenge for content managers and for creatives to make sure we're nailing it.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:59): Yeah. What are Norco's brand pillars? What do you guys consider?", "token_estimate": 527, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "05:48", "end_timestamp": "07:59", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "1c5b18eb-5f5e-49ca-86be-a7478468ec85", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Evan Poitras (08:04): So we kind of went through a big rebrand about a year and well, it started two and a half years ago, but it came out in March, which is super exciting. It wasn't necessarily changing our brand, but more I would say making it more coherent both internally and externally. And so what we landed on really first and foremost was our BC roots and our backyard, and the fact that Norco has been here since 64, and if you look at the evolution of what, I guess you could call it recreational cycling has been since 1964, has changed dramatically and is continuing to change with e-bikes, right? It's just constantly in flux. But Narco has kind of been there at every point, whether it was banana seed to the seventies or BMX racing in the eighties, or I think everyone thinks of the shore, the 45 pound bikes, all skinny is huck into flat there then. (09:05): So yeah, it's sort of the first one we always think of. Those scenes were always happening in this area as well, and it was such an influence to the brand. And so with Vancouver being probably the most cycling dominant city as well, it's just part of our nature and who we are. And so trying to sprinkle that in even when we're not doing stuff in DC to keep that front of mind as our birthplace. And then our second one we use is more of our design and product philosophy. We just use the word uncompromising to basically encapsulate that our design team, they're never happy, good or bad, they're constantly pushing the envelope. And that also reflects in the athletes that we choose. They're constantly pushing the envelope and just never being satisfied and trying to do better and better and do more things and change it up. And sometimes they're just throwing what's been, oh, this is great, but we're just going to throw it in the garbage to start a new plan. We think it can be better. So that's our third one or our second one, sorry. And our third one is freedom, and it's a generic word, but it's that sense of being out riding in the woods that we all love mountain biking for. So we'll unpack those differently, but those are kind of the three terms that we try to be latching onto with everything.", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "08:04", "end_timestamp": "08:04", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "9199b4bd-232d-4040-bc93-d89a5ecd9377", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:35): Yeah, those are great. I love the background info on each of those, but so when you're coming back to content, when you're putting out a piece of content, is it your job to make sure that whatever it is is meeting all three of those brand pillars in this piece of content?\n\nEvan Poitras (10:53): We kind of balance them. Say we don't try and hit all three at the same time because there's certainly a different feeling of being in the start gate at UCI downhill. That is not freedom, that is as stressful as it gets. But on my content calendar, actually, if I could show the people it's literally next to the title, it's the title and what theme are we hitting? What are we trying to tell here? And so it's a balance in how we sprinkle them in or out or we focus on one and kind of shift it and change it. But yeah, it is kind of my job to make sure we're covering all the bases and with the balance that we have created with our new rebrand and our brand guidelines and everything to make sure we're kind of driving that vision forward equally. So we're not all just engineering or not all just trail running in the woods. We're building this picture that we've set out to build in an equal but all encompassing manner, which is a challenge, but it's a fun one.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:58): Coming back to the original point you made, which said either you've got one chance to communicate who you are, and I'm just thinking of, let's use a photo, for example, if someone sees a photo, they need to understand who Norco is, or someone sees 10,000 photos and they need to understand who Norco is. So considering those three brand pillars in that one photo example, how are you approaching the content and the creative and the design of that photo to encapsulate everything that needs to be communicated?", "token_estimate": 456, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "10:35", "end_timestamp": "11:58", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "82e3d4bf-5304-4d2b-a091-0ff9f03df7cd", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Evan Poitras (12:30): Yeah, certainly. So this is where our creative manager and team comes in a lot of the time, specifically to help me out here. They have the eye and more importantly, they have the coherence to explain to say a photographer what needs to happen. Whereas I'm just sometimes like, yeah, it just needs to be more of that. I don't know. I don't know how else to say it, but there's certain things like we have set tones we want in our photos, what kind of hues we want to come through, what kind of style we want to shoot. We have shots from both shoots we've done in shoots that aren't ours, and we bring them forward in a photo brief. When it comes to product, that one gets detail oriented to the nines because it has to do with everything from the cranks need to be level, it has to be shot from this side, not that side. (13:24): The cables need to look a certain way. The chain needs to be on gear four because of how it bends, the derailer, all this kind of stuff because we want everything to look a certain way for us. And so we have to set all these things out beforehand and really work with them to make sure that we get it set that way. But we have everything from how we want our, I'll just use the classic putting on the goggle shot, every mountain bike film ever has, but how we want that shot, if we're going to use it, I try not to use it, but if we're going to do that, this is how we would do it. And if we're going to use still of someone having a beer at the end of the day, how do we want that still to look? How is it framed and lit and everything? So it gets pretty full on pretty quick, but I think the more every shoot we do, oh, we should add that one in for this category. Oh yeah, that one's good. And you start to build it and you start to get a rhythm. So certainly when we were trying to shoot in the lead up to the brand, I think it was definitely a lot more of a loose cannon, and now I think we're really hitting our stride with what exactly we're looking for. So\n\nCole Heilborn (14:37): Do you have the same level of detail that you're looking for in a bike shot that you do in that post beer ride shot or that goggle shot?", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:30", "end_timestamp": "14:37", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "7f859862-0544-47ec-b94c-dc43932f939b", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Evan Poitras (14:46): Maybe not. It depends on how, for our studio bikes, it's way more, way more particular. We're very, very particular about how we shoot them by studio bikes, I mean what we're going to use as the product shot on the website and all that in our studio at HQ in park Whitlam, but it's not quite as detailed. There's just not as many factors. But generally we try to give a photographer the direction and try to be, push yourself on this one, try and outdo that or with that angle. And so maybe not as much upfront, specific direction, but there is still inspiration, direction and feedback after. So it's all pretty involved. But generally speaking, these are pretty full on conversations beforehand with the photographers and so there's no surprises and usually we're picking people who we think can hit those marks anyway. If we see someone who is just, they could be great, but just very different, we might just be like it's, we don't know if it's going to work. So there is that too. You have to sort of pair yourself with the artist who can match it.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:01): So when you're considering photos, you're getting that specific, which I think is interesting. A lot of, I mean, I would just assume sometimes brands are like, oh, we got a cool photo. Let's put it up on Instagram. But you're going through it pretty meticulously, making sure that it checks out", "token_estimate": 349, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:46", "end_timestamp": "16:01", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "56885d4f-a5ff-4bb1-959d-82d36ad2a03f", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Evan Poitras (16:18): To a point. We're a little bit more loose with social media and just the volume you need to be growing too. The goals we have on there, but for an instance, got an email last week, some person just sent in some photos on our web customer service and they get forwarded on to us and stuff, and it was like, whoa, they hit all the tones. Those tones are perfect. That's exactly what we want. So it's like, Hey mate, dude, can we have these photos? Can we purchase these? That was they the greatest action and the best photos ever? No, but the styling that this particular photographer got, just the way they were shot was like, that's exactly what we're looking for. And I wouldn't say that we're overly specific that we're cutting people out. Certainly we've drawn inspiration from other people to make these guidelines. So yeah, it's not all the same all the time. But yeah, we are kind of looking for that. If someone submits something that's way off, it might be we will hum and haw on it if we really want to use it or not, but\n\nCole Heilborn (17:25): Yeah. So when you're looking at a photo and you're like, does it match the tones that we want? Are you trying to match the tones because that matches a brand color guideline or does that match those? Is that somehow line up with those three brand pillars that you're mentioning?\n\nEvan Poitras (17:41): Yeah, I think it's all actually intertwined into some capacity. It's more that visual direction we've pulled out of our brand pillars and tried to bring forward that inspires the brand color guidelines and all that. So in a little bit of ways, it's all connected and just part of how we're trying to build the new brand for Norco. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:09): So on the flip side, how do you consider what types of content to pursue? Because you could also look at the medium of the content and say, does this fit our brand or does this not fit our brand?", "token_estimate": 484, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:18", "end_timestamp": "18:09", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "e039cc2e-1bb2-40a7-bb9d-3c5ea46a06b0", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Evan Poitras (18:22): Yeah, so Medium is a really interesting one to me because typically it's all just photo video and the length of copy you attach to that may change. I'm always sort of thinking, is there something we can do that's not photo or video or can we turn it on its head? And I'm not going to tell you have any answers right now, but it's certainly a place where we're going to explore and test and try and see what we can do. I think things like gifs obviously kind of blend the two animation can do that. You can kind of play with things. But yeah, the medium of the content really in the more realistic standpoint comes back to, okay, where are we going to find our audience and what's going to hook them? I mean, that's what you have. It's hard to explain sometimes why we can't get so creative, but when we come back to where the audience is and what we know works, you have to balance that too of sticking with the audience and knowing the tried and true and pushing. (19:26): You can't always just do whatever you want and hope people see it. You got to go know what people will see. So there is a balance of that that we work on and we kind of play with, and you could say, I negotiate with different people. I kind of sit in the middle. Sometimes some people want to be super creative, but if we only get so many, how amazing are those going to be? And some people, we just need to make sure we get to them. I kind of ping pong between the two sides a bit, but in reality we do tend to, for major projects, come back to where's the audience we're after looking, what are they looking at and how can we get in front of 'em and get a meaningful sort of engagement and not just a scroll past or ignore. So yeah, it's an ongoing challenge, but it's a fun one and one that I think is going to just continue to change as the world changes.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:23): Sure. Are there any mediums specifically that you're looking at and trying to feel out?", "token_estimate": 492, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:22", "end_timestamp": "20:23", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "d51b77f5-2854-4b24-843d-9e569bdd8f12", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Evan Poitras (20:30): I don't know if there's any specifics right now. I could say there's been a lot of dabbling in thoughts of what we could do. Some brands in the industry are doing their own podcasts. There's things like, I mean, print be back in an interesting way. We've sort of maybe mentioned that maybe I'm giving too much away, but could that be back somehow the same way vinyl is back in an interesting way. And then kind of anything in between different playing with different mediums. Even on say an Instagram, there's so many different ways you can actually post a video if you really dig into it, what you could do with a video. And you can do quite a few different things. And so that's actually one we're working on right now for something in the future is how many different ways can we play with here and still make it all work and make it useful for the campaign, but also test within this one little, I mean it's our primary source of volume for reach, but inside just that one tool, there's a variety of ways you can do things and what works and what doesn't work. (21:42): And so that's always kind of ongoing and one we're certainly working on right now just to get ultra specific, but then you break that out to every other way you can communicate with people, it becomes quite a lot. So we try to pick off little bits, work on a little bit at a time.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:01): Sure, yeah, that could quickly become, you have your own development team for new mediums and new techniques I suppose.\n\nEvan Poitras (22:10): Yeah, totally. Totally. I mean, we're talking about other kinds of things right now that I probably shouldn't, I just don't want to leak them. They're quite a ways away, so I don't want to, but it's like what would it take to do that and take this to that level and yeah, it's like a team.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:28): I'm curious if in the past you guys have ever published content and after it's gone out, the reaction you've received from your audience, you're like, oh shoot, that definitely did not fall in line with who we are as a company. I feel like you can have the best parameters, but sometimes you only realize what your parameters are by screwing up.", "token_estimate": 551, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:30", "end_timestamp": "22:28", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "9aa9b97f-047a-415e-a9e8-51e6de722c28", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Evan Poitras (22:51): Oh, definitely. It's hard to say. I think we've had some stuff that people, maybe it just didn't land. I don't know, because before we set out on the rebrand journey, it was in need of a brand alignment to begin with. So it was kind of hard to say whether or not we were landing. I don't think people knew what we were. And for the people that maybe don't know Norco as well, or they know us for the premium bikes, we make all kinds of different bikes. So there's kids bikes, cruiser bikes, step throughs all over the place, all kinds of different bikes and have been for a while. So some people weren't sure what to think of, so maybe they weren't replace it. Now that we've sort of aligned ourselves and gotten ourselves sorted, maybe some stuff hasn't quite landed. I don't think we've heard that's off. (23:45): That's not Norco yet. We're probably still too young into this process to hear that, but we've had stuff go and we're like, oh, that's what they picked up out of that really. That's okay. Step back. Okay, what did I not see in version one through 11 that maybe had this comment strain going? I didn't think that's what people would pick up on. Obviously you're at the mercy of the loud minority on those ones. But yeah, there's been some interesting ones where we publish stuff and you're like, oh, I can't believe that's what they're hanging onto right now. There's so much more to it than that or people just kind of not getting it. But it's always, I love it because even in my personal life, constantly optimizing is how I get referred to by my significant other constantly optimizing. And so any kind of info I can get written down below, I liked that. I didn't like that I don't take it personally. I'm like, okay, next one, we're going to hit that mark. I love that stuff. So you're right, you can't learn until you screw up, but you've got to just roll with it. I think for us, for me and my time at Norco, most of it's just been a surprise of some little thing that I didn't think was significant and people are hanging onto it. It's like, okay, well next time we got to clean that up or make sure that's really on. So", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "22:51", "end_timestamp": "22:51", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "a5881101-8f43-48fd-bcbd-f5f3e36370af", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:11): All of this is, none of this really matters if there is no impact. So let's say you put something out there and it's not in line with Norco and the community responds to it. Part of the question is, so what does that really matter? Does it matter if something goes out that doesn't match up or line up?\n\nEvan Poitras (25:33): I think mean, yeah, you could say it doesn't matter. I would like to think that it kind of does, maybe not as drastically as you might think, but you can't let the ball run away from you. You can have things every once in a while. It's the same with branding in general. If you're building a strong brand, you can have a bump in the road, you can handle it and it's fine, but if you're already shaky, that bump is going to blow the wheels off. So I think if you're doing it well, you have that room to sort of get experimental and see what happens. As long as people know what you stand for. I think there's some other brands in the industry who are pushing it, and I love it personally. I know there's a lot of comments that don't like it, but I'm like they're putting a stake in the ground and getting weird with it. And because I think they've done a great job at branding up until this point. They have that bank account to draw from, say or however you want to look at it. But if you're really shaky to begin with and you're not really clear who you are, you probably don't have as much room to be messing about.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:43): Yeah. So one example that comes to mind is the whole Toyota photo thing earlier this summer. I mean, I think that's a good example and maybe if you could explain for us what that example was, but does that, if we had to guess, does that damage Toyota's brand?", "token_estimate": 438, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "25:11", "end_timestamp": "26:43", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "dca87daa-f510-4c59-8742-57d04fce9f04", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Evan Poitras (27:04): Yeah, so if people aren't aware, the photo was, I believe it was an Instagram ad they released, that's where I saw it, and they had a truck with a person going mountain biking, and this person's in full motocross gear, not mountain bike gear riding, what can only be described as a Walmart bike, and it's just a tough look. And the tagline was something about for those who get outside and get after it, I think that's a tough one. I think in that case you kind of laugh about it and you can move on. Toyota's actually done quite a lot of different avenues into mountain biking. Maybe people don't even remember, but they've done some stuff with avenues like, well, they're doing it right now with pink Bike and they've done stuff with athletes specifically maybe in bc. They see that market and they've somehow built themselves as, if you live in north, you don't live in Vancouver unless you have a silver Tacoma type truck with an arteric sticker on it type thing. They've built themselves as that. (28:07): Some of it's luck, some of it's just the product they have and some of it's they've made actually some pretty good decisions in the past. So you kind look at that one and you have to laugh and wonder which agency got fired for that, or if anyone there even noticed you as someone who loves the outdoors, would hope that they consulted somebody who loves the outdoors and before they just go off and do things like that. But yeah, I think it hurt for a bit, but they have done some stuff in the past and to me they're uneven, they're fine. It was tough, but they'll get over it.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:51): So obviously, I mean theyre such a great example, like yeah, as you said, are you even a mountain biker if you don't have a Tacoma driving up to the trails? So there's this deep expectation, I feel like almost of what a mountain biker should be, and that is what a mountain biker should be. So as you said, they have a deep bank account that they can draw on from this brand, but how many of those photo, let's call it a blunder, would it take to devalue it enough to a point where Toyota no longer is the mountain bikers' vehicle? Does that make sense?", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:04", "end_timestamp": "28:51", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "fa557757-6689-4feb-a27c-09cfe584266b", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Evan Poitras (29:34): Yeah, I think it is probably going to depend on the person. I think some people might be more offended by it than I was. I mean, I've worked with brands that have big agencies in big cities and they do great things, but yeah, they're going to miss the mark sometimes because they're just not part of it. I think once is fine, but you got to make sure that is, you learn from that. If they can show that they learned from that, if they show that they didn't, then I think it starts to become a problem. But you got some very clear feedback very quickly, and that's got to go up the flagpole. I think showing you're not listening to people or not learning from that is where you get into trouble. So if they did two posts in a back to back Tuesday, Wednesday and no one realized or picked up, that's a bit different than doing an ad six months later again with the same thing. So that's where I think people might start to be like, okay, you're really just not paying attention to who you're trying to market to right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:44): Why is that important though?", "token_estimate": 274, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:34", "end_timestamp": "30:44", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "57687429-6d7c-42c9-a2f4-818ec568c1b8", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Evan Poitras (30:46): I think one people, especially with outdoor sports, maybe sports in general, I would say they take it so personally it becomes a part of who they are. So if Toyota's going out, here's a mountain biker, and you're like, no, that is not an accurate representation of what I do or who I am or anything. I mean, we've all tried to describe mountain biking probably to our parents or uncles at Christmas and they don't understand it. You ride down the ski runs with your bike, it's a thing. And so we all take these sports and we identify to them, oh, you're a mountain biker and a skier. Okay, I get you that. It's so personal. It's not like you bought cotton socks and this is everybody who buys cotton socks. It doesn't matter if you're not associating that with a brand. I think people just take these activities and lifestyles so to heart and you can't kind of devalue them, make them feel devalued in your marketing material when you're trying to be like, we're with you on this. And whether that's just ego, fashion show of every consumer of, I don't look like that. I don't ride that bike. You still have to be aware that that's kind of part of the sport. People do appreciate if it's mountain biking, like having the gear and having a nice looking bike and all those things, and they want to identify to that. So if you go out and tell them your identity is something different, they're probably not going to be too happy with you.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:23): Sure. So from the Norco perspective, do manage, how do personally manage all the content that goes out and how do you make sure that it's up to code? Are you reviewing everything? Is there a process in which you could share that you guys make sure that everything is legit?", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:46", "end_timestamp": "32:23", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "d27afd1a-6b2c-477b-8531-ecd67716eba6", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Evan Poitras (32:43): Yeah, so I dunno if I want to call us a big team or a small team, maybe. We're a medium marketing team and we have a content team. We have a creative team. Our creative team has three designers including a creative director and a creative lead and a graphic designer and a copywriter. And so for us, the process really begins with, for most things, it becomes a layout. This is a brief, this is what we're trying to do, this is subject matter. Here's the idea. And then depending on how developed that idea is, me and the creative manager probably talk about it for a while, to be honest, who's going to work? Should we do it? Should we not? Do we kind of pro and con budget is always a reality, and then it becomes shooting it, and then I go away and work on the distribution plan and go back and then it filters through our creative team, whether it's already been written, if something had been shot and written, it will go through them and myself for feedback and go through our copywriter for the written part. And it kind of filters through that team and with outputs from both myself and our social media specialist to make sure we're hitting all our marks. So yeah, it kind of probably runs by, I would say at least five people before it gets out. If it's something bigger, maybe six if we turn it to email and seven if it's web. (34:21): But that's pretty much like our whole team. So everyone mostly for the most part, sees it at least on the content and creative side. Everyone will see it and have a chance to feedback. So yeah, sometimes if it's just, Hey, this photographer submitted these photos to us, fire 'em over. Usually I'll fire 'em over to our creative team, be like, are you billing any ads he worked for? We can hold them. We won't post 'em on Instagram if we're going to make an ad a month later with it, they're usually like, oh, can you hold this one? Boom. We mark it as hold and we run. So yeah, depending on the project, it always filters through myself and usually through either our social media specialist, if it's just strictly a social play or if it's anything more than that, our creative manager certainly dives into it and puts his eyes on everything and make sure we hit those details that he's looking for.", "token_estimate": 568, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "32:43", "end_timestamp": "32:43", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "ce32d3f3-a0c7-48fc-9132-ca95c4e1ef58", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:13): Is there ever a point where content becomes less important or we use less of it?\n\nEvan Poitras (35:19): That's a really good question. I think, yeah, it's a tough one to answer because everything during COVID just ramped up to content only. I think we're going to appreciate in-person experiences a lot more shortly or whenever we get to do that again. And I do think nothing does be word of mouth, but I think where content plays is you're trying to subtly increase your equity with the people who are going to be talking. It's an analogy I always use because we have a range of bikes. I always say our top end model sell our mid end model because the person who's buying the, I have $2,000 and I want to get a new mountain bike and I'm getting back or I'm getting back into it, or whatever is going to ask about with the 8,000 bike. So that word of mouth to me is always going to be the most important thing. (36:21): But if you can subtly start to build up some equity, certainly if I wasn't working at Norco, I would have brands I would recommend to people to look at, but you've got to build equity with everybody. And so I don't think it's the most important thing even right now, but I think those human touches and when we get back to events and experiences, and certainly we have an athlete, Jill Kitner, who is just magic at making people's day out of nowhere and they send us comments and things about it, like she showed up and just had a coffee with us at Crank Works, Jill, they're mind blown. And those things will always be the most powerful, but what we're trying to do with content is try to bring those in the intermediary times. Here's something to watch. You like it, you connect with it, that's a great story. You like that character or that theme or whatever we're telling, try to get there. But I think the human side will always be more powerful, that personal connection or the person you trust answering your question or the person you look up to, you engaging with you or whatever. I think those are always going to be the biggest. You can't make those up either, right?", "token_estimate": 531, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "35:13", "end_timestamp": "35:19", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "5e78653d-56c0-443e-aff6-1696a678b042", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (37:36): Sure, yeah, good point. What limits do we place on content?\n\nEvan Poitras (37:42): Man, that's an interesting one. I think it's currently quite unlimited. I think I'm going to plug one of your past episodes with my good friend Andy Bethune. He did take a, I don't want to say he took a shot at the mountain biking industry, but he called the spade of spade, and it's a very engineering driven industry. It's very geometry, spec, kinematics. And while all that stuff is important to some people, not a lot of people or maybe a large portion of mount markers aren't necessarily as obsessed with those things and the pure technical side of it. And so I think there is room for doing things differently. I think we've started to see it with brands, certainly, I think, I wouldn't say we're doing things too crazy differently, but for us, we're building up stories of a specific set so we can try and get these emotions of riding across the people and connect with them on that level. (38:47): But I think of a brand like yt. I mean, they did a 35 minute movie about this goat cult to launch a bike. I mean, that is the director directed Peaky Blinders on Netflix. I mean, when that came out, I was so excited because they just moved the bar so far in one move and some people hated it, but it's like, well, okay, now we're doing 35 minute episodes of fiction to launch a, where are we going next? And there's all kinds of different things that people have done and brands have done, and biking is behind in that aspect. I would agree with Andy. We are behind in that sort of level of branding and storytelling. It's such a young industry and it's an industry sort of created by engineer and product people, so you can't blame 'em. I mean, I certainly don't have the brain to design a bike, so I wouldn't expect them to be able to do it all either. But now that brands are really investing in marketing and storytelling, I think we've got a long ways to go before we're going to run out. (39:57): I think of all the things that are going on in other industries, just around the boom of streaming and different live sports things, red Bull TV being the obvious one, there's just so many different platforms out there. Every once in a while someone's going to make a crack in it and something new is going to flow out. So certainly in our industry, I think there's a long way to go both in terms of how, like we said, medium and what you're doing within that. So lots of room, lots of room. Hopefully I have some job security on that one. I think there's no end in sight.", "token_estimate": 647, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "37:36", "end_timestamp": "37:42", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "2b6ca903-a70c-429f-a5a5-d1cba7cfe03e", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (40:35): Do you ever feel like the mountain bike shred, it will go away. I have mixed feelings about this type of content, but I'm curious to hear your thoughts here.\n\nEvan Poitras (40:46): I don't think so, and I kind of hope not. And here's why. I'm going to use an example from our side just to plug Norco. We did a video, oh, just about two years ago now to launch our new optic with Burn Atkinson. It was shot by Scott Secco and the whole concept was brought forward by those two, I can't exactly remember which one specifically, but they worked in tandem on it and it's essentially, it's a shredded, but it was quite successful. And the reason it was successful is it was shot differently or the premise was different. The whole thing was just about routing your bike fast. But what Scott and Brian were able to do was take inspiration from actually Robert Downey Jr's, Sherlock Holmes, where he slows the boxing match down and they translated it to Bris slowing down the trail at a thousand frames per second and breaking down what he's doing in narration. (41:45): You're in his head hearing what he's thinking and to people who'd be like, how is he thinking in that much detail? Honestly, he is. It's true. The guy's, he on another level and they brought that forward. It's just riding a bike. But the way they shot it and framed it was so different that it was unbelievable that I hope there's more of that how we take riding a bike and just show it a little differently and frame it or whether it's how you shoot Slowmo, no slowmo. I mean, raw 100 is a great example. I remember reading All they Wanted was no sound and no slowmo, and that was the briefer raw 100 because everything at the time was heavy metal and all slowmo, and they were just like none of that. And that became Raw 100, which is one of the biggest mountain bike branded content series out there. (42:39): So I hope that people keep going in that direction because the of the day it motivates me to ride my bike and my friends to go ride. And when you're out riding, you want to try and ride that person or emulate that a little bit, but I hope the creativity and how they bring it forward continues to go. I think some brands or people are still really doing the generic, set it to a heavy tune and show some shredding. And if that works for some people, that's great, but I hope that evolution of how we show it continues because every mountain bike ride is so different, so there's so much to capture and ways to do it. So I hope it stays with that caveat that people get keep getting creative.", "token_estimate": 648, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "40:35", "end_timestamp": "40:46", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "33be413f-01bf-4a1b-aa41-279588cde4f1", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:26): Okay, that's a good distinction. I did not make that in my question, but I appreciate you making that. That piece is a great example of a shredded that could otherwise be average, but it's phenomenal with the creativity that was applied to it\n\nEvan Poitras (43:42): Without the Phantom 1000 fringe per second and the brand narration. That piece is blah for sure, but it's how they did it. Was it great? And I still get people texting me like, oh, hey man, just to watch the optic video. How good is that? It's like, man, that was two years ago. Can we move on to some of my more recent work?\n\nCole Heilborn (44:06): So you mentioned YT as an example. What other companies are breaking some of these prescribed limits?", "token_estimate": 185, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "43:26", "end_timestamp": "44:06", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "06805b62-d6d9-40c6-89a4-292e707b6b98", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Evan Poitras (44:17): I like to think the first one might've been, maybe not the first, but the first one to really own it and they keep doing it is Shiano with this is home. They just kind of left product in the dust and just told human stories. Gosh, seven years ago they started and they're still doing it now. And I think that's just a really good testament to how successful that's been. They basically just started telling the human stories of their athletes without even showing a shot of a derailer and it's keeps working and they keep doing it, and they keep upping their game too. They're getting better and better. (44:53): Obviously, brands with bigger budgets are more front and center in that world. I hate to call it the big red S, but love 'em or hate getting different and they're doing interesting things, whether you agree with or disagree with it using actors. I think they started using actors before yt, but having actor driven things, they've done some pretty cool stuff. Who else? I'm trying to think of a brand that isn't one of the big, big ones that's been doing some cool things. Gosh, I'm just trying to rattle rack my brain here. I usually have so many of these off the top of my head. I'm just on the spot. But anyone who's kind of taking something and spinning it differently in my mind is winning. Whether or not I agree with the adder or what, maybe just because I'm a big content nerd, if you try something new, I am okay if it doesn't land because new. (45:59): So if you want to be specialist and have monsters come out of the mountain and fight with, I can't even remember what the premise of their latest e-bike ad was. Everyone, I'm just like, you know what? I'm down. I'm in. That was cool. You gave it a shot and you committed to it. So I kind of tend to remember those big ones just they stick with me and I watch. I think, yeah, there's some other stuff, other brands, I mean, there's some brands that I've had ideas and like, oh man, we really should do that. And then they go and do it for us. So maybe they're not recalling in my brain, but they've executed well and it's like, oh, okay, that was actually sweet. I never would've thought of doing it that way. I've had this sort of idea and they kind of took it, but they did it differently than how I would do it, and it was actually really cool.", "token_estimate": 586, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "44:17", "end_timestamp": "44:17", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "01962d9d-b9d0-40ca-abcb-c9cdad5b713d", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (46:50): What about on a simple scale, on a much smaller scale that everyone could participate in? Who's trying something different or what are you guys trying differently for that matter?\n\nEvan Poitras (47:01): I'm just trying to think of how we would frame athlete or brand or, I'm just trying to think of who I watch on a daily or weekly basis who I try to look to.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:16): One that maybe comes to mind is, and I don't know how, maybe this isn't a good example, but Eric Porter has its YouTube channel and he's a Diamondback athlete, and I love his stuff so organic. It's so chill. Also, he was on the podcast, so people should go listen to that episode.\n\nEvan Poitras (47:34): They should go listen to the Eric Porter episode. I'll back that up. Even though it doesn't ride for Norco. It's all good.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:41): I don't know. That's the first example that comes to mind.", "token_estimate": 229, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "46:50", "end_timestamp": "47:41", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "869af5df-49d9-4289-9b2b-3c903efbff09", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Evan Poitras (47:44): I think, actually, you know what, I'm an a shameless plug here. I think Jill Kitner is doing something very different. People may know Jill Olympian five time recruit, crank work. She raised World cups forever. She done everything and now she did a contest with Red Bull, and then we kind of jumped on the back of it to do a kids' version because Red Bull and kids don't work. But it was all her idea where you could color and she would draw these templates. She has this very specific art style that she's developed, and it's super cool. It's kind of entertaining as an adult, but it's also kid friendly. It's all very nature based, and you could color them in and win a contest. And we ended up supplying a bike that you could design and win. And what Jill does with her art, I think is without giving anything away, because it's not our project, she's got some cool stuff coming. (48:40): I think she's done a complete 180 that anybody else is doing. From an athlete side, at least in mountain biking. I don't pay too much attention to other sports just because there's so much mountain bike stuff to keep track of every day. I think she's doing a great job of that, and as someone who is just so dominant at what she does on the bike, doesn't have to do that. You know what I mean? But she's doing it anyway, and I think that's super cool. Yeah, Eric's definitely one that was pushing and kind of blending that YouTuber with actually being a pro rider. Louis Buchanan on a roster does that quite well. He kind of bounces between going to races and events and being a YouTuber, but he brings you on for the ride, which is cool. (49:26): Yeah, there's a lot of that of people doing interesting things. It's hard to pull out the highlights, but I think on the athlete side, there's definitely some ones that have figured it out how to mix the two. And if you're winning World Cup downhills right now, you don't need to. And that's fair. But there's some other people who have brought something else to the table and it's really neat. I remember, okay, I just thought of one. Marin, I believe did a video with one of the writers in Scotland, and she played the score on the piano for her own video. And to me, that's a just plus one in my book any day. That is so new, you wouldn't think of it, but wow. And I remember just thought of that one as a great example of, yep, she's riding in the Scottish Highlands, it's been done, but one, she's a good writer, and two, she played the soundtrack on the piano. (50:27): That's neat. All of a sudden now I want to know more about her. How does she play the piano like that and ride a bike? And that's pretty incredible. So yeah, there's definitely things like that, that one that doesn't cost you anything. You already sponsored the athlete that small brands can get in on and bring to life and do things like that. So that's a cool one. I'll shout out Marin, even though they're a competitor, they did good on that one. I'm almost positive it was Marin and I don't want to say the athlete name. I'm probably going to get it wrong, but it was about a year ago.", "token_estimate": 782, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "47:44", "end_timestamp": "47:44", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "cf23a288-074f-4b48-b07a-310f80b1d725", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Cole Heilborn (51:00): Okay. Right on.\n\nEvan Poitras (51:13): Yeah. I guess the one thing that I always say, and maybe I don't want to be preachy here, but the one thing I would say is Alana read in a book, and I'm going to forget the name of the book right now, was that people don't learn from being told facts. They learn from stories and pulling the narrative away. And that's where content needs to be like you need to be telling the narrative. The boy who cried wolf is don't lie, but it's told through a story that you understand why you shouldn't. And so that's what content needs to be, in my opinion, and less literal. And I think that's where the endless of what we talked about is paramount, because there's so much that with biking still, there's so much room left to tell narratives that have a subtle underlying premise to take away. And so that's what I always have that one written out. It's on my desktop, just read it. Okay, how can we communicate something by telling a story that entertains people? So that's kind of my go-to one that I look at and think of every time, but,\n\nCole Heilborn (52:15): And just making sure that it all matches up and aligns and you don't post the wrong photo.\n\nEvan Poitras (52:22): Yeah, yeah. We try to make sure we're pretty good, but I mean, for the most part, that part becomes second nature and pretty easy. So yeah, keeping your brand tight is my second goal, is always keeping the brand nice and tight and consistent. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:39): Well, Evan, thanks for sharing the time with us today. If people want to follow along with you or with any of the projects that Norco has going on, where can folks go to connect?", "token_estimate": 422, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Evan Poitras"], "start_timestamp": "51:00", "end_timestamp": "52:39", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "8a609dd3-ebbb-45f0-8430-1c4868dade2b", "episode_id": "72eb48f5-ab7c-4a11-9822-3e0cee9662dc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4871", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Evan Poitras (52:48): Yeah, you can probably, if you want to connect with me on LinkedIn, don't follow me on Instagram. I spend too much time making Instagram content for Norco that I don't have time for my own channel. It's weak. So follow Norco bicycles on Instagram or YouTube, and if you want to chat content, you can find me on LinkedIn. Just use the name in the episode. I shouldn't be too difficult to find. And yeah, keep your eyes on Norco bikes. Hopefully we got a bunch more entertaining stuff coming your way in the near future.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:17): Awesome. Sounds good. Well, thank you again and hope you have a great rest of your day.\n\nEvan Poitras (53:21): Yeah, thanks Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:22): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 235, "speakers": ["Evan Poitras", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "52:48", "end_timestamp": "53:22", "podcast_index": 75, "episode_name": "EP 75: Evan Poitras | Aligning the Intricacies of Content with Brand | Norco Bicycles", "slug": "ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-75-evan-poitras-aligning-the-intricacies-of-content-with-brand-3ac26", "guest_name": "Evan Poitras", "guest_title": "Content Marketing Manager, Norco Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "Your content and brand need to align.", "podcast_length": "44:97"}, {"id": "8025c4fc-006d-4564-9232-8261126c7ce1", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (00:10): One of the realizations I had to quickly come to was that I'm not building a brand in North America. I'm just exposing North America to a brand. So you have to take all of the steps of a more established brand to exist here. You can't sidestep or skirt or shortcut the way a startup or a super, super scrappy two-year-old brand can.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo, and today I'm sitting down with Roberto Gutierrez. He's the director of brand Marketing at Jack Wolfs Skin. Roberto, how are you doing?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (01:15): Doing well, how about yourself?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:16): Doing great. Thanks for joining us. Where are you streaming in from?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (01:20): I'm in Salt Lake City right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:22): Okay, right on. You got a mountain bike lap in earlier this afternoon, so you're feeling fresh and recharged for our episode, huh?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (01:29): Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:30): I love it. So let's kick things off. If you could tell us a little bit about you background and how you ended up with Jack Wolfs skin. I know you've got quite the background, but let's try to condense it as much as we can.", "token_estimate": 452, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:10", "end_timestamp": "01:30", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "2855f3fb-d4ee-48ff-b3c7-3deda2f88e4a", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (01:45): Sure. So the short version, I guess, is I started in academics after PhD, got disenchanted with that for a bit, pursued a little bit of the startup world in the Bay Area. Then ultimately became co-founder at ORU Kayak and built that up over the course of six years, stepped away to do some other equity crowdfunding type things. Then got into the outdoor sports marketing specific realm in at United Sports Brands with a group of brands, and now I'm helping to lead the reintroduction or introduction of Jack Wilkin to North America here under the Callaway brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:26): And somewhere along the way there, you also started a van conversion build out company, is that right?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (02:32): Yeah, my cousin and I founded a little van conversion company based in Boulder, the Van Smith, and it's doing really well. It's pretty fun,\n\nCole Heilborn (02:41): Man. That's quite the background. And I got to wonder, with all those experiences, why make the choice to take on this director of brand marketing role?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (02:52): I think it's a great question. I think it's a couple of things. There's the brutal honesty of some stability associated with larger corporations, and then there's also, the way I explain it best is I've helped build, I guess 20 million or 30 million brands, but when I one day run a hundred million dollars brand, I want to know how that works. And that's sort of the hope and aspirations of it, is to see the inner workings and learn and glean from that. I know it's a bit of a pipe dream, but hey, I got to put one foot in front of the other, and what better way to learn than under the hood and get paid to learn?\n\nCole Heilborn (03:27): Sure. For folks who are unfamiliar, could you give us a background on the company?", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:45", "end_timestamp": "03:27", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "3d52cf30-5084-49fb-b1a4-b67d3c4cbb14", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (03:34): Yeah, so Jack Wilkin is 40 years old, founded in Germany. The inspiration actually came during a camping trip in the Canadian Rockies. So the founder was camping with a group of friends, heard wolves crying in the background. He had a fondness for Jack London and the Call of the Wild sort of combined those two concepts and aspired to build a very sustainable, eco-friendly and high performance product line that was centered around hiking. Again, the Love for Wolves and Jack London. He combined the two and got Jack Wolfs skin, a very German name, if you will, the combination of the two in that nature. But the company grew for 40 years and is one of the largest brands. Well, it is the largest brand in central Europe. It's one of the largest outdoor brands in the world. Huge global distribution, 1500 doors globally. We have two in North America soon to be more, but not very many at the moment. (04:32): We actually are opening with public lands in two weeks, so we're pretty excited about that. The only final things is our brand is interesting in that when everyone was talking about sustainability, we just were doing it. We had the opportunity to work with Gore-Tex, but they said, well, we don't have the transparency we hold ourselves, hold ourselves to. So they developed their own membranes and rain protection and all of that named it tech support because they wanted to know each and every person along the way and have full transparency where sourcing it didn't allow for that. So it's an interesting thing where we've just been doing it, and so it's just core to what the company does. We actually came up with the world's first fully recycled membrane or waterproof and breathable membrane.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:18): Wow. And I think I read that, was it 2000 or early two thousands? All of the manufacturing facilities switched over to Green Energy In Germany?", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:34", "end_timestamp": "05:18", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "7f9e029f-1bc3-491e-9181-e5f11062eee3", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (05:29): Yeah, in Germany, yes. And then one of the coolest parts, honestly, is the level of transparency. So inside of every piece of apparel, you can look and look for a little white tag, and there's two numbers. One is the source. So you can find out where the materials came from. You can see the full pipeline. You just enter that number into the search bar of our website, and then the second is the manufacturer, and it'll take you to the address and sometimes even a picture of the facility and people who built the product for everything we make, which is really amazing.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:59): Wow, why do that? That's a lot of work.\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (06:04): Again, I think it was from the very beginning, Jack Wolf, the whole Wolf skin concept was they wanted something that was as somebody that had as little impact on the environment as possible, like a wolf skin, which is all natural and all that. That's kind of the core driving inspiration. Of course, you can't be perfect. No one's perfect in that regard. We try. We try and be good or better and even best. So it's just been something that they really hung their hats on. When I say they, I mean the design team and the developers and all that. And it was just something they decided they were going to be uncompromising about. And so we as a company get to represent that. And yeah, it's pretty subtle, but it's very cool.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:46): Yeah, no kidding. And what a cool opportunity. So you mentioned that the brand has been around for 40 years. It's been based in Germany, but the brand is recently entering the North American market, and that's kind of when you got involved, correct?", "token_estimate": 422, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:29", "end_timestamp": "06:46", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "1c7388dd-da6f-4712-b4ff-9d9ed3993e8a", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (07:01): Yeah, correct. So 2019, Jack Wilkin was acquired by Callaway Golf, and prior to that they had done some really, I would say, rudimentary efforts to bring Jack Wilkin to North America and blamed it on brand or blamed it on fit or color that it didn't succeed. But honestly, they didn't ever necessarily invest to the extent that we are now. So we have a team and a headquarters here in Park City. We've got global support, new CEO, and we're really aligning on a global level from product to messaging to ambassadors to all of it is becoming much more of a global brand rather than having the Poland market and the Chinese market and the Japanese market and then Dock or the German Central Europe market, and then North America, it's now all branches of the same tree. And you'll see that realize itself over the course of the next couple of years.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:57): Is that common, that unifying global unification?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (08:04): It's a great question. I would say yes. So I mean, we've seen f Raven Heli Hanson and then going overseas, the mountain hardwares and Patagonia, north Face is branching out, and you have to have localization. Our top seller here is not the top seller in Germany. The color stories that win here may not win there, so you have to have those components. And then in Europe, in Germany in particular, trekking poles have to go on everything, but here, nobody, it's like 14 trail runners who have carbon fiber trekking poles. It's not true, but it's just a little different. So we have to try and retell those stories. But globalization is, I mean, it's obviously relatively new as the internet is relatively new. I know for all of us, it's a commonplace thing today, but in the nineties it wasn't early nineties it was pretty relatively unique. And so as the years have passed, it becomes more and more fundamental, and it's only 30 years ago, but still,\n\nCole Heilborn (09:11): You mentioned that previous attempts to bring the brand to North America were unsuccessful. Can you elaborate on why that was?", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:01", "end_timestamp": "09:11", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "bf5fdc22-2537-4fa5-93a6-af3694a9a131", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (09:18): Yeah, it was a couple of things I think that they did. It was a mix, from what I've seen. It's hard for me to speak on it because I wasn't here for it. So first, lemme caveat that, and I'm sure the folks who put the effort in did do some highly strategic and hard work to take those efforts. That said, I think that it was a little bit of a, they were on an island, and so when they came over here, it was like, okay, figure it out and just report back on what you need. Here's one of everything. Tell us what sales. Well, that's pretty hard right now. You sold one thing and you have a hundred of everything else, it doesn't sale, so it looks bad on the books, but really you just had the wrong stuff. And so it was a mix of inappropriate investments in locations and processes. (10:01): And now we've got Diana Song, who's our general manager here, has a background in merchandising. And so a number one, she's helping to select key items in strategic manners so that we don't sit here with 900 hot pink jackets that nobody's, or 9,000 that nobody's ever going to want. Instead, we have a bunch of the styles and colors that people do want, and then we dive deeper in that. So I think it was maybe done haphazardly without a, yeah, maybe they're pushed out of the nest a little early type thing. Like, Hey, in Germany, if everyone knows Jack Wilkin, it's like, oh my gosh, what a brand. So they just assumed that that same status would be carried over. But American, the North American market is very competitive.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:51): Yeah, that's an interesting point. What are the differences between the two markets or cultures that influence things that you're considering?", "token_estimate": 428, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:18", "end_timestamp": "10:51", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "c7a26a28-6f54-47be-b5ca-b2d8baf54507", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (11:03): Yeah, I mean, I think that's what we are trying to do is how best to answer that rather than reacting to exactly where we could fit. We are choosing to try, we haven't done it yet, but we're choosing to try and carve out a space where we feel we belong. So we think that in our space and in this space in general, there is room for what we like to say the difference between an adrenaline brand and an endorphin brand. And so we're a brand that helps promote that moment of awe, but it doesn't necessarily have to mean that you're hucking off a hundred foot cliff in Jackson Hole, Wyoming doing a back flip or something. That's awesome, but that's not what we want to tell a story about. And so it's, ours is a little bit of a changing perspective where our pinnacle athlete or adventurer is a Nat Geo photographer who's holed up in a blind trying to take photos of a rare wolf in the Arctic. (12:06): That person still needs epic gear and has done something really amazing, but they don't, and they're at a pinnacle in one way or in a different way, but they're not a gravity sports expert. They're not going to slam a red ball, throw it off, and then go pro their day. That's not us. And so we're trying to carve out that space of and helping people change perspective and found everything in education and fact, or at least debate. And that's what we're trying to do. And so we believe there's space in that, not just in the outdoor world, but also in society for people to hopefully search fact or search for. It's hard to say that I'm biased, but hopefully at least do research and trust science again, because science has definitely fallen out of favor.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:59): When you say promote fact and truth, what does that mean? How are people finding those or discovering those with the brand?", "token_estimate": 459, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:03", "end_timestamp": "12:59", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "7cd1ea03-001e-48b7-b427-d93dff71fabb", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (13:08): Well, I think what we'd love to do is, I mean, this is as much me expounding on my personal objectives as it is trying to represent the brand, but I would love for us to be a meeting ground, a commonplace for people to communicate. So for example, we're doing a bunch of, we're doing an embedded mini documentary on Wolf trackers in Yellowstone National Park. They are based in Montana. It's a really fascinating story. These two, Nathan and Linda from Yellowstone Wolf Trackers have been there basically as long as the Wolves sensory introduction. And so we get to hear their perspective, their stories, and they're very objective of it. They understand both sides of the arguments. They certainly are passionate about wolves and have a very special relationship with them. And I won't deny that they are willing though, to sit down with anyone and discuss it. (14:03): They aren't, and I think when we talked about this program originally, people like, well, we need to talk about why we protect the wolves, why we carve out their space a keystone species. I was like, awesome. But let's also bring farmers, ranchers and those who don't believe that the wolf should be protected to the table. Because if you present one side of an argument that's just a bias or an opinion, if you bring both sides, then people can actually make a decision based on an argument and let people make that decision. So in that realm, what we're trying to do is bring things to the table that we believe are, and we're going to support the things that we believe once we've listened to the facts or the right action. I mean, that's all you can really do. But we'd like to bring, I guess, an open table, if you will, an open conversation.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:56): What does that say or how does that reflect the brand? How does that reflect the values of the brand?", "token_estimate": 468, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:08", "end_timestamp": "14:56", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "66228663-62e3-4717-a846-ff7594f2a1c5", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (15:01): Yeah, I mean it's everything. Like I mentioned, the sourcing is a great example of that, right? It's just something the brand does. They're transparent, they understand that not everything's perfect and easy, and it takes a lot of hard work to accomplish the things. So I think that it really resonates with the, I don't know if it's grassroots or I don't want to say blue collar, but there's that upstart mountaineer, if you will, who's going on their first hike and is like, man, I did 2000 feet of elevation today. Awesome, great. Congratulations. That's a huge accomplishment. And then a few years later, they do rim to rim to rim or something like that and throw down 12,000 feet of gain in a day and cover 22 or whatever, 44 miles I think that is. So yeah, I think that it's that gateway to the outdoors. There's the back country and the front country. We're kind of the middle country, if you will, and we'll build pinnacle product and we'll build entry level product, but most of your consumers will lie somewhere in the middle.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:09): So there's this concept, I believe, called confirmation is bias coming back to this concept of presenting all sides to an argument. And I'm curious from a philosophical perspective on storytelling, how you would approach something like this, because I think the concept basically says if you give equal weight to all sides, then the audience assumes that all arguments are weighted equally, even if there's more evidence for one argument versus the other. And the example of your film, how do you think through something like that?", "token_estimate": 405, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:01", "end_timestamp": "16:09", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "358b3c9e-9087-4d20-b7d6-c04ac0359d14", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (16:47): Well, I have a bit of a background in science, right? I've got a PhD. So I look at numbers and try and look at the bigger picture and how it affects the whole story. So for example, there's an argument that the wolf needs to be protected, a number one, because it's a keystone species. Well, in talking with other researchers, so a keystone species, it could only possibly be a keystone species if left unadulterated by any outside forces, including man. So as a result, even in Yellowstone where it's very close to it, man's still feeding elk, and man is still hunting this, and there are still false perimeters and roads, and it is not untouched by man. It is as close as we get in North America right now. Well, as in United States right now, lower 48, but it's not untouched. And so taking that into account, the other side will argue, well, they're not necessarily an alpha, or sorry, a keystone species man is a keystone species. (17:50): Well, the good news, bad news there is that that means the responsibility is on us. So it then reflects on us to take that into consideration. That said, wolves in the greater ecosystem make a very positive impact pretty much every level. And so the research does indicate that it is statistically and ecologically more sound to have the wolf there and to protect it and restore it. That said, there's the caveat of it's not a blanket, just do it 100. No, no, no. You have to be considerate of the fact that there are people who also cohabitate in these regions. And so that's all it is, is it's not taking a blank, a one-sided blind approach, walking blazing a path through the woods. That's how we got here in the first place. But taking into consideration that this is complex, we have a goal, and to get that goal done, it's going to take some work.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:50): Yeah, no, well said. The art of telling a story in itself, the science behind it is it can be frustrating and very complicated.", "token_estimate": 495, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:47", "end_timestamp": "18:50", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "176bad68-d88b-40ac-b0c0-c17883b28d40", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (19:03): And I think what's interesting, so I was doing research on, I should have done the numbers so I can bring it up, but the number of cattle killed each year by wolves is far lower than the number killed by domestic dogs and then killed by coyotes. Yet we don't have the same visceral reaction. And so the numbers indicate that if you're going to use that as an argument, you have bigger fish to fry, right? Vaccination, like sick cattle is one of your number one killers. So if I were to take all the money we're spending to fly helicopters in and hunt wolves, maybe that could be better spent vaccinating cattle. So there's just interesting conversations to be had all around. I think that the hard part is then, as we mentioned earlier, with all the fake news and people sort of finding their own sources or finding their own information without checking the sources is becoming a problem. And that's what we'd love to highlight. But it's tricky. It's very hard to do.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:09): Well, it sounds like an amazing project. When will it be out?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (20:13): So we're going to release a teaser in hopefully two weeks. So keep an eye on our social media, Jack Wilkin sub score Na for North America, and then the little mini doc will be released at public lands in, I believe Pittsburgh in December, and we'll be offering a chance to win a free trip to Jackson Hole and then go track wolves with the wolf trackers. So keep an eye on that. Yeah, it's a pretty cool giveaway or sweepstakes I guess.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:45): That's awesome. That's super cool. So kind of jumping back to the work that you're doing day-to-day, bringing the brand to North America, what differences have you had to embrace just between the German brand and the brand here in North America? Are there differences that you're having to account for and correct for?", "token_estimate": 472, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:03", "end_timestamp": "20:45", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "b1a44bfc-a8ae-41b0-ad7b-0a9b9ca52e28", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (21:06): Yeah, I mean, honestly, there's a couple of things. So this year is our 40th year 40th anniversary of the brand in Germany. That means a lot, but for us here in North America is what we would call one of our first years in North America celebrating your 40th year is that's like a for year old showing up to a one year old's birthday party and be like, I'm 40, nobody cares. And so we just have to adjust some of that messaging so that it's more relevant to people being introduced to the brand rather than, Hey, it's our 40th birthday. It's like, Hey, we're 40 years going. And so it's little things of that nature. And then I think one of the things we honestly struggle with is as a company founded in hike and trekking in Europe, that is just a thing you do in North America. (21:56): We've got this kind of, I dunno if it's cavalier or cowboy mentality, where everything has to be done to its pinnacle and peak. And so as a result, your fastest hikers or your best hikers are either mountaineers, rock climbers or trail runners. And those are different sports. We don't make climbing gear specifically, we make stuff you could climb in. So one of the interesting things we're trying to do is, but on the flip side, most people are out hiking, more people are hiking than they are summiting, even Whitney or Baker or whatever. And so yeah, we make the product for the people. It's just that in the ecosystem here, people have an expectation. So we're trying to build awareness, and that's been a tricky one without having, that's been probably the biggest thing I've struggled with is trying to identify that pinnacle piece. And then of course, our sustainability. In today's day and age, you have to be cautious because people just read it as greenwashing. It's like, no, it's who we are. And it's not like we're hanging our hat on, it's just what we do. So we find some obstacles there, but at the end of the day, the product is premium and fantastic and well priced, and so it'll carry itself. And once we are able to tell more stories like the Wolf Tracker one and other stories like that, I think we'll be fine.", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez"], "start_timestamp": "21:06", "end_timestamp": "21:06", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "7c2c1498-62da-4bbe-b6be-f9a3574ae418", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:30): So you're saying that you're having to, the challenge is trying to embrace the North American pinnacle perspective on sports, or sorry, maybe I'm not tracking with what that challenge is.\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (23:43): The system is built a certain way. So the influencers, storytellers, marketeers, adventurers and so on are all shaped around a few core stories. And then you tell your big stories about something special in that realm. So North Face has a stable of rock climbers and mountain bikers and trail runners, and they have a couple in there who are your Alex Hos or your Stephanie Howes of the world who are going to be pinnacle trail runners, things of that nature. So the system is built that way. So if I'm going out to find our pinnacle hiker, our person, it's tricky. The Pinnacle hiker is it, like I mentioned, Stephanie Howe, right, who's a phenomenal trail runner, probably not, right? She's somebody who averages like 10 minute mile for a hundred miles. That's not even through hiking, that's another level. So I'm probably a nine minute mile for that long, honestly. But anyways, yeah, it's just a little different for us there. Right.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:55): Why is that difference? What does that difference say about the culture difference between North America and Germany? Could you speak to that?", "token_estimate": 336, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Roberto Gutierrez"], "start_timestamp": "23:30", "end_timestamp": "24:55", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "91c61bf0-7784-4c2b-9a04-0eac198fdd28", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (25:04): I think if anything, the culture difference is the, so to give you an example, and I hope I don't get thrown on the bus for this one, but so Columbia, right? My first camp first all probably, sorry, yeah, it was maybe a Columbia tent and a Coleman stove. If you were to show up at the base of Whitney with a Coleman stove, you'd probably get laughed out, right? You'd be like, dude, you're doing what now? And so then Coleman, because there's this pinnacle perception gets this B grade backyard old person camping vibe, whereas if you showed up with that same heritage trusted piece in Germany, you would be seen as like, well, that's a wise decision. That thing works. And so some of our tents, we have some lightweight ones, but the majority of our tents are actually quite heavy, but they are phenomenal. Whereas in the US it's like grams, grams, grams, grams, grams. (26:08): And so you just get this like, oh my God, that thing just works. That's epic. Whereas in the us, we'll fight with a pole until our nose is bleeding because we want to save three grams. And so I think there's probably the difference where you can still stay. I don't think it's going to be very hard for Coleman to ever regain a prestige back when the canteen or whatever it was was probably their biggest thing early on, that it'd be hard for them to gain prestige where in Europe and in other places, being quality and sustainable and durable earns you that same prestige. Just culturally, it's appreciated differently, I think, if I had to say.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:51): Okay. Interesting. I feel like though there's a space opening up and welcoming brands who aren't the classic elite Summit Pinnacle brand. Would you agree? Do you see that as well?", "token_estimate": 440, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:04", "end_timestamp": "26:51", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "26425165-7cf4-481f-a3c7-a13e0c78e782", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (27:09): Yeah, absolutely. I mean, you've got what, Cotopaxi and Peak design, sorry, not Peak design, Topo design and Snow Peak. I guess Snow peaks pretty pinnacle at times. But yeah, there's absolutely a space for those brands and it's being much more welcomed into this space. I think they also have a bespoke feel where you can have the founder of Cotopaxi Davis is going to stand up and he's going to say something and talk, and he's going to be on their ad and he is going to post on their LinkedIn where the founder of Jack Wilkin, I think retired 20 years ago from Jack. You know what I mean? I don't know the exact dates, but there's a difference. And so I think that yes, it's just we're a slightly different flavor.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:01): Sure. What do you see as the biggest opportunity bringing Jack Wolff skin to North America?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (28:07): I do love the what Jack Wolff skin bikes keeping everything proprietary has been able to do is bring high quality product at a better price point. And so I think being able to introduce people to performance like an 800 fill down at a price point that they could for 150 bucks instead of 350 bucks, that opens a lot of eyes. And to sort of be the gateway there, I think that that's our opportunity. And then also to celebrate, as I was talking about those endorphins over adrenaline. So I always like to give the example of you can look at a grass field, just stand there, and it's a big field of green, but if you lay down on your stomach like you used to do as a kid, you'll see dew drops and bugs and the blades of grass look like it's something from Honey I shrunk the kids. And that even just changing your perspective, that is it five, six feet laying down, it's a whole nother world. And I think that we can celebrate those moments without having to set up an illuminated tent over a boundary line so that you get a good Instagram picture. And so I think because the world sort of has been waking up to that just for Instagram and Red Bull type, Huck Go big mentality, that was not a slight on Red Bull. They're a fantastic brand. But", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:09", "end_timestamp": "28:07", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "6545e676-694c-40d2-9977-31de4839c6a2", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:30): You've talked about some of the challenges to bringing the brand in North America. Is there one looming threat that kind of keeps you up at night?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (29:39): It's probably self-imposed. If anything, the expectation. I love the saying it takes 10 years to make an overnight success, and I think that we have to keep realistic expectations for each day and everything we're doing. We're not going to be sponsoring the climbing wall at REI tomorrow. It's not going to have our logo all over it, but hey, one, that's not the goal at all. But two, we just have to be realistic in those. We will do amazing things. The stories are going to be incredible as we go. We're definitely going to invest in a global level. The impact we make continues to be great. So we're doing all these amazing things, but like I said, it could be a minute before we're a household name, so we have to keep that in mind.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:29): Is that conflict, is that friction for you personally, because you're all about getting out there, making stuff happen as quick as possible?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (30:37): Yeah. Yes and no. I think eight years ago it definitely would've been, but now I think that knowing where you are is really important to figure out where you're going. You look back twice as far as you look forward type thing, but it's tough without a huge history. So the hardest part is celebrating the wins. We did more revenue in two months or three months this year than we did the entire year prior. That's incredible. But that seemed like it was off the mark because our forecasts were supposed to be higher than that, and it's like, okay, well keep in mind and celebrate those little goals. So I think it's just a, and then we made it up in the next month and it's like, oh, wow, we didn't expect that win. So it's kind of back and forth on that, and it's just trying to keep perspective, what is it? Can't see the forest from the trees type thing. So yeah,", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Roberto Gutierrez"], "start_timestamp": "29:30", "end_timestamp": "30:37", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "43b0ddaf-dfb8-4734-925c-772e86af5d6c", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (31:38): This might be a really long answer, but how do you even begin to bring an established brand to a new continent and just say, oh, tomorrow we're going to give it a go. How do you even begin that?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (31:54): Yeah, I mean there are a bunch of answers to that. So you can check the y'all Raven model where they brought a couple items over and then their backpack just flew off shelves and they basically built a brand in North America on 25 different colors of the same product. That's really boiling it down. I know there's a lot more that went into it, but that's kind of what they've been come to be known for. And even their site North America is backpack heavy. So that was one way is to pick a couple winners and carve out your space there than introduce more and more of the brand. I think one of the realizations I had to quickly come to was that I'm not building a brand in North America. I'm just exposing North America to a brand. So you have to take all of the steps of a more established brand to exist here. You can't sidestep or skirt or shortcut the way a startup or a super, super scrappy 2-year-old brand can, so there's no pivoting there.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:00): Why is that? Why can't you take those shortcuts?", "token_estimate": 317, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Roberto Gutierrez"], "start_timestamp": "31:38", "end_timestamp": "33:00", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "f4797da4-7be8-4f6e-833a-6013a51b8fcd", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (33:03): Well, I mean, again, Jack Wilkin is a global brand whose product development line is being built out years in advance, any other global brand. And if all of a sudden we see North America is like, oh my gosh, our bike touring line is just blowing up. We should focus solely on bike touring. Well, the company may have only made a certain amount of bike touring or planned for a certain amount of bike touring. We can't just be like we're now a bike touring brand because that's not what we are. We're a whole outdoor brand offering sleeping pads and tents and shoes and socks and hats and beanies and jackets and layers. So because it's big ship, small rudder, we can yard on our rudder as much as we want, but it's still going to keep going straight. So we have to sort of address that, and then you just have to address, you either have to change the boat or change the destination. And so if we're not changing the boat, we just have to adjust our expectations.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:08): But I mean, that's not always a bad thing. Being able to show up with the strength and the resources that you do probably makes a lot of startups be like, well, shoot, we would trade some flexibility for 40 years of history.\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (34:23): Yeah, no, I mean, yeah, no, coming from the world where crap, are we going to make payroll this week? Hey guys, how much money do you have in your bank account? Can we pay our team to, man, this healthcare program is really nice here. They're different, those types of things. I think it's just frustrating for people who you put a players in a position where they're not allowed to sort of, and this goes across our team. We have a really, really talented team and not allowed to blossom at times or to flex all the muscles they wish they could. That gets hard. So\n\nCole Heilborn (35:01): Yeah, that makes sense. I know we're kind of coming up on the end of our time for today, but a couple more questions. How have people received the brand? Do most people know that it exists here?", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:03", "end_timestamp": "35:01", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "421a54dc-dabf-4b48-bb05-e316a7ff2ab0", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (35:13): It's very binary. It's either, I've never heard of this, or Oh my God, I saw that in Europe one time. I would say in the outdoor space, it's like 20% is aware of the brand. Outside of that, it's like 5%. And I mean people working in the outdoor space, they've been to ISPO in Germany and seen the ginormous Jack Wilkin booth. But no, it's been phenomenally received. Honestly. Sometimes the zippers are on the other side, and so some people are like, wow, why is this so frustrating? I can't zip it with my left hand. But you get used to it after about three tries, and so it's little nuances, but the jackets are still amazing, or they're like, holy cow, this is next level and this is how much, and you offer this in three or four different variations with a hybrid, a fleece hybrid. Yeah, so it's been really well received. The story is definitely catching on. We have a solid presence in Canada now that continues to grow. So I would say it's been really well received.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:19): That's great. And what's your hope for the future, both for the company and also for you personally? When we first started this conversation, you mentioned that you're excited to be doing this job, kind of learning some of the under the hood work that goes into a large company. What are you excited for?", "token_estimate": 333, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:13", "end_timestamp": "36:19", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "d44f7603-5cc9-4249-b5dc-e366acefef32", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Roberto Gutierrez (36:40): Well, for Jack Wilkin, I'm excited for, so we're in a change state, so we've had a launch. Then we had COVID in the middle of that, which changed our entire distribution strategy. Then we had a bunch of a turnover as a result. Basically people who were like, well, I wanted to do this, but now you're doing this, so I'm not a fit type thing. And now we've kind of got the team and direction aligned, and it's just sort of like that wobble. But as soon as the wobble's done, I think it's going to be really cool to start blowing goals out of the water. We've had a couple of our biggest direct consumer months, and we're just primed for some really awesome success there. The stories are just being unveiled, and it takes time to make that happen. This Wolf Tracker thing started in January and it'll be done in December. (37:31): So I'm excited for all of those things to come to fruition and us to have a new cadence. And then, yeah, I dunno, man. For me, we'll see this is, it's a pretty cool gig, so we'll see how far we can take it. It's amazing to be part of a, it's not a rebrand, but a brand adjustment and the globalization of, like I said, I think it's the fifth largest outdoor brand in the world. And to be able to help at least sit up with the captains and share and influence the direction, that's a pretty cool job. So we'll see where that takes us.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:10): Yeah, no kidding. Well, cool. If folks want to follow along, where can they go to connect? They want to watch the docuseries that's coming out. Where can they go to learn more about that?\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (38:21): Yeah, so it'll be@us.jackskin.com and you'll be able to find the docuseries at Jack Wolf Skin Na on social media. So Facebook and Instagram at the moment. And then it'll also be on the global site, which is just Jack Wolf skin.com. So keep an eye out there. Definitely look the Yellowstone Wolf trackers. We will be working with them quite a bit and then expanding to other groups actually in the next year, which we'll talk about next year.", "token_estimate": 518, "speakers": ["Roberto Gutierrez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:40", "end_timestamp": "38:21", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "412cf899-96ec-4522-92ea-74d4a01fd78c", "episode_id": "cbe3b0ea-80ec-4ce2-b2b0-50d78992d111", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4873", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (38:52): Sounds good. Well, Roberto, thank you for the time today, and I wish you guys the best of luck as you're making some headway. Sounds pretty cool.\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (39:00): Thanks. I appreciate it. Thank you for your time. I always enjoy doing this.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:04): Absolutely. Well, we'll chat soon. Take care.\n\nRoberto Gutierrez (39:06): Sounds good. Bye.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:09): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 156, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Roberto Gutierrez"], "start_timestamp": "38:52", "end_timestamp": "39:09", "podcast_index": 76, "episode_name": "EP 76: Roberto Gutierrez | Bringing Jack Wolfskin to North America | Jack Wolfskin", "slug": "ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-76-roberto-gutierrez-bringing-jack-wolfskin-to-north-america-jack-wolfskin", "guest_name": "Roberto Guiterrez", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing, Jack Wolfskin", "categories": [], "short_description": "Bringing a 40-year-old brand to North America.", "podcast_length": "44:98"}, {"id": "af71ebaa-5b52-4225-9517-f6564e0f674f", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Al Perkinson (00:03): I believe that it's our job as marketers to build a tribe of followers and a tribe of believers. And that when you're marketing what you're going out and looking for other people who believe what you believe. And if you can build a company and build a brand around value system around shared beliefs around emotion, then you always have to do everything else right as well. But man, you've got people by the heart, you got 'em by the soul and they will fight for you.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn. Today I'm sitting down with Al Perkinson, he's the founder and CEO of Bao Sunglasses. Al, how are you doing?\n\nAl Perkinson (01:14): Doing great, Cole. How you doing buddy?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:17): Fantastic. Excited to be chatting with you on this Monday. How's your day going?\n\nAl Perkinson (01:22): It's a Monday, but considering it's going pretty well, I'd say going. Great.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:27): Well, I'm glad to hear it. Let's dive in. Can you tell me a little bit about Bao? Who are you guys?", "token_estimate": 414, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:27", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "c34f6780-a9d6-4fc5-882b-b6f3393b9cbb", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Al Perkinson (01:34): Well, we're a pretty new young company. We've been around for grand total of about four and a half months. We're based out of New Smyrna Beach, Florida, and we're an independent company. We make our own stuff here in our little, it used to be a race car garage and out in the country, and we moved in and sort of revamped it a little bit and are doing our operation out of here, but we have a lot of people who are from this area and had worked for another sunglass company that's no longer here. And so we've got a great team of friends and about 15 people here and probably another 45 out scattered about the country. And we make performance sunglasses primarily for the fishing market, but really for any type of water activity, everything we do is very sustainable, sustainably made, the materials, the processes, so we're real proud of that. And then we use what little cloud we have at this point, but is quickly growing to make a difference in the world in terms of ocean conservation primarily.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:45): That's awesome. And I'm assuming fly fishing and being on the water is a passion of yours?\n\nAl Perkinson (02:52): It is. My wife, Marguerite and I, when we were starting this company, we knew that it was going to be 24 7 and we said, Hey, what direction do we want to go? And said, well, we got to do something that we love and it needs to all be about that, so we'll have for the energy and the passion to take to make it happen. And so we love salt water, fly fishing, that's our big thing, Marguerite's from out west, and so we love western fly fishing as well, but the top of the top is bonefish and tarpon and permit on a flat and some beautiful place in the Caribbean or wherever else. So that's what we love to do.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:37): And bajo is Spanish for shallows, is that right?", "token_estimate": 461, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:34", "end_timestamp": "03:37", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "e7c75ab0-79db-434d-8c16-a5a6860898b8", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Al Perkinson (03:41): That's right. So the flats are that shallow park that's sort of between the land and the sea. It's beautiful turquoise and brown and blue water lots of times very shallow, but sometimes tidal as well. And so that's what our friends in Mexico call it, but it also applies to other shallow areas like the marshes around Charleston and up and down the east coast or the bayou of Louisiana, but it's that low country where it's just teaming with life full of really cool stuff and great fishing.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:18): I was looking at your YouTube channel before we were jumping onto this podcast and it surprises me, you said you've only been around four and a half months. I was looking at all of the content that you guys have on your channel, and I was like, wow, these guys, you guys are doing some pretty cool work. And then to hear that you've only been doing it for four months, that was surprising to hear. That is surprising to hear.\n\nAl Perkinson (04:40): Well, we jumped right in and got started a little bit before we launched, but we've been here before and it's a content driven world. It's all about storytelling, and so telling the story of the brand or about the causes that we're interested in, about the people we love, all that needs to be done through film and photography and the creative arts. So it's just essential these days that that's a part of what you do from the very beginning.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:11): Well, I'm excited to dig into our episode today. We're talking about the five components of an authentic brand. Before we jump into that though, could you give us a little background just on your history in the industry, how we ended up here,", "token_estimate": 425, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:41", "end_timestamp": "05:11", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "3d8f5e00-54dc-4018-8d96-2cf6d974fee6", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Al Perkinson (05:27): Finn and in marketing and advertising for my whole career? I started in New York at Ogilvy years ago and started working, was in the agency business and got a little sunglass company in Florida called Costa Sunglasses As a client when it was a $6 million company, they asked me to come over and head up marketing, which I did for about 16 or 17 years. And we grew that into a pretty sizable brand and really did it with a lot of cause-based type of marketing and with an emphasis on brand authenticity because it at the time was a cool little authentic brand. And so I think that's a little bit what we're going to talk about today is sort of what the formula is for that. We were successful, I guess it was back in maybe 14 or 15. We were named one of the top 10 cult brands in North America, and that was up there with Converse and Yeti and Patagonia and some of the big guys. So our little strategy worked pretty well and happy to share it with some others.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:40): Back in the day when we connected, we were talking a little bit about this topic, and you mentioned 20 years ago or so, you and your team were trying to break down what does it mean to be an authentic brand really before I think the authentic thing became a buzzword, right?", "token_estimate": 321, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:27", "end_timestamp": "06:40", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "1e68d68f-7d76-4182-b7da-c1b1d668a242", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Al Perkinson (07:00): Yeah, that's right. When I first met Costa and it was a little fishing brand out of South Florida, the thing that struck me about the brand was that it was authentic, that it was a brand that was used for a specific purpose by specific people, and there was just kind of this, for lack of a better word, authentic nature to the whole deal. And so we were charged with developing the brand strategy for them. We kind of got hung up on that idea of authenticity and started asking the questions, what does that mean? Actually, you hear people say it from time to time, but what does it actually mean And what other brands are like that we might want to look at? So I think we looked at probably 40 or 50 different brands that we just said, I think that's an authentic brand. And that was everything from Hershey Chocolate to Chick-fil-A, Patagonia. (08:09): The list was of types of companies and types of product was really broad. But I remember we got this big spreadsheet, like an Excel spreadsheet, and we started putting just different things about each brand and each of the little boxes. And then after a while we looked at it and things started lining up and we were like, oh my gosh, have these authentic brands all been playing from the same playbook, all doing these four or five things that are the same? And so we feel like we felt like we had discovered the holy grail. And so we took those five things and just kept working and thinking about 'em and refining 'em and then use those as sort of the basis for the brands we were working on. And it just worked. It resonated with consumers and resonated with importantly people within the company who just sort of intuitively related to those things.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:20): And you mentioned that you just made a list of 40 or 50 brands. I'm sure folks listening could probably do the same. They could look at a roster and just pick brands that they feel like are authentic. What about a brand makes it feel authentic? If you had to do a gut check on 10 different brands, you could pull out the ones that you feel are legitimate. What goes into that process as consumers are trying to identify authentic brands?", "token_estimate": 549, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:00", "end_timestamp": "09:20", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "23bcf916-942d-45b0-9aab-c1e0e7d90785", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Al Perkinson (09:50): Well, I think you start with just kind of a feeling maybe that this is a real company, it's a real, it really is about the company as much as it is about the product. And when you get into authenticity, I think you have to look at the whole thing. You can't just look at one piece like a product. And back in the day when packaged goods were king and there was Proctor and Gamble who's still around, but there was this whole sort of detached management of these brands, but the company behind the brand was never really brought into it. Nowadays, especially with the visibility into companies that the internet has provided, it's hard for companies to do that to create a product out here while the real company pulling the strings is behind it. And so they're held accountable and things kind of have to be in alignment all the way through. (10:56): But I think you start out feeling like this is real, and maybe it's a company with a really cool story. You look at so many brands now, and it wasn't like this. It wasn't as prevalent 20 years ago, but now every brand has figured out how to craft that story of two dudes who started out as kids love to do this. And then that interest grew into a passion, and that passion grew into a business. So you hear that creation story, and from the beginning we believed in this and we still believe in that today. So that creation story is something I always look for in terms of brand authenticity and then a higher calling or a purpose as well in the brand where you're about something more than just commerce, you're about something more than making a buck. Consumers really expect companies to be given back and not just taking resources or natural resources or people's money, but that there needs to be sort of a balance. (12:09): And so there needs to be what we call it, a higher calling as part of that authenticity story usually too. There's a different look and feel. I mean, design has evolved to be such an important part of brands. Brand design is a whole discipline, and there's some great new brand design companies out there who've perfected it, but that design, just like your higher calling, it says something about your values. And if you look at a brand that's really simple and it's design scheme might be kind of primitive, that probably says something to you about what kind of company that might be. If you see one that's super high tech looking, then that's probably, you're a high tech company, but it should get at some deeper values as well. And then once you get out and start operating in the world, you standing behind your product, just like the dairy farmer did with his cow and his milk, you standing behind your product is super important. And then really being committed to your customer and treating them a part of the family and not just as somebody you're trying to sell something to. So those were the kind of four or five things that popped out at us, and I'm sure they're recognizable to everybody.", "token_estimate": 752, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "09:50", "end_timestamp": "09:50", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "3bd0388b-ec28-4969-8e85-d881e87c32b9", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:39): So I want to jump back and let's talk about this idea of a creation story. I'm curious first, what is your guys' creation story? Because what I find interesting is you're brand new four months into this, and so I'm sure a lot of these points are things that you guys are asking yourselves as you're in this development process, but why is this creation story so important?\n\nAl Perkinson (14:02): Well, I think it kind of seats the brand in a specific place, and it creates a set of, I think mostly a set of brand values that, and it gives those values legitimacy. You can say these were here from the very beginning, and these are part, not of a marketing strategy, but part of a passion strategy. It's something that we really, really do believe in. We're not faking you guys out. This is always what we believed in. It's always what we will believe in. So there's so much skepticism about value stories that planting them in the creation of the company and even beyond that and the creation of the founders of the company, I think it's super important. And it's kind of like with a person, I think marketing, branding, it's all about being human and a brand is a human. It's like a person, it's an entity and it goes to work every day. It does stuff outside of work. It has a belief system, it has friends, it has enemies. It's sort of an organic being. And so if you're asking a friend lots of times the first question you ask when you meet somebody is like, oh, where are you from? Why do you ask 'em that? Because where they're from probably gives you some clues about who they are. If they're like, Hey, I'm from Texas, man. You're like, okay, he is a Texas dude. Or Hey, I'm from California. Hey, he's a California dude. (15:55): And it's not just like geographic place, but you hear people all the time, whether they're a musician or an actor or politicians, especially football players. I was born in a log cabin and I didn't have anything growing up. So I learned how to be scrappy and I'm still scrappy to this day. So yeah, I think those creation stories just help inform people in a legitimate way about who you are and what you care about.", "token_estimate": 544, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "13:39", "end_timestamp": "14:02", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "c62f2c48-aac3-42dc-a052-526d345e5d84", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:27): Could we not look at creation stories though and be like, well, how true really is it, are those stories just being made up to further legitimize the brand and question?\n\nAl Perkinson (16:40): It's that way kind of across the board because marketing people are really smart, and when they see something that works, they want to copy it. The good news is though that the internet has become kind of the great leveler and the great investigative tool. And so usually the truth, I think it comes out and it is hard to fool all the people as to what the real underlying truth is, but it is definitely attempted. I think there's all kinds of brands, and we were doing some work with some folks in Nashville, and I started reading the bios of all of these country stars and their publicists wrote, they wrote these stories and these stories are not true, but they help that they know that that particular singer, that their audience is looking for a certain type of person, maybe it's like a strong few words, man's man. And so they try to pattern their story to fit that narrative. And consumer brands do the same thing. So you question it, you research it, which a lot of people do these days, and you try to find out what's really real.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:12): What is your guys' creation story? How do you articulate that?", "token_estimate": 336, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "16:27", "end_timestamp": "18:12", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "65e0bb74-3315-4230-acce-9f87f8dffc05", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Al Perkinson (18:16): Well, other people kind of create it for you sometimes. And several of us worked at Costa Sunglasses for many years and built that brand. And then Costa was bought and we felt like we built Costa into a very likable, very friendly kind of brand a few years ago before all this happened, people would say, they wouldn't say, I like my sunglasses. They would say, I love my sunglasses. And when we would do loyalty scores, they would be astronomically high. So there was this cult following that was built around goodwill, around good customer service, around quality products and all of that. And so part of our creation story is coming from that. And what happened was a giant corporation from Italy and half of us from France, half from Italy, bought that brand and laid off 300 people in this county. And so we've come in to give those people their jobs back and more importantly to the industry needs a brand that has the values that we personally hold and that we've had before. And so we feel like it's important to have a brand like that in the industry. So that's the short version of that is, hey, the old Coasta guys got the band back together and started a new company, and it's kind of supposed to be like the old one was. So that's how that gets translated in retail among people, I think.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:09): Okay, very cool. And that leads us then to the second point that you mentioned, which is having a higher calling standing for something beyond just commerce, as you said. I know you guys are heavily involved with a lot of conservation, environmental programs. What else? I feel like that's a very common higher calling piece for brands in the outdoor industry. Are there other forms of a higher calling?", "token_estimate": 438, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:16", "end_timestamp": "20:09", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "27a1489f-61cf-49d8-b331-2db328e9f432", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Al Perkinson (20:41): Yeah, there are. It's interesting that the role that brands are playing in our society these days, and not to overstate it, but brands are kind of taking the place of the creators of the mythology. And it used to be that religion was probably the biggest driver of mythology in society, and that's probably not as true anymore. And mythology is super important to a society because these are stories that we tell each other that tell us how we should live our lives. And so there's a moral to the story and you're supposed to learn from it. And that's the mythology when we're talking about higher calling. You ask yourself, what is the moral of our story and what is the message that we want to put out in the world that's going to make that world better? So there are acts that you can do, go roll rocks in a stream or go plant a tree or whatever, but there's also sort of philosophies and mythologies that you can put out there. (21:57): And there's so many, there's so much emphasis these days I think on the narrative of money and that if it creates jobs, it's good. If it makes money, it's good. And it's the excuse that so many people use to do bad things. And so you'll see a company, a corporation that'll come in and destroy the environment and be like, yeah, but look how many jobs we created. Well, how many future jobs did you threaten or eliminate by doing what you're doing? So anyway, I think that sort of the narrative that you put out there and the mythology that you put out there can certainly be a form of higher calling. And one of the messages that we want to put out there is to the millennials and the Gen Z folks to say that money is not the end all be all. It's important to make money and support your family, but don't sacrifice your life because your parents told you you got to be responsible and go make a bunch of money, live your life based on what you're passionate about, live your life based on something that aligns with what you love to do, and it's okay to do that. (23:27): And so it sounds like an odd message to be coming from a brand, but I think it's a totally a legitimate one. And one of the things that we try to emphasize is exploration. And you don't know who you are until you've gone out and explored and put yourself through the paces and put yourself in situations that are scary where you're uncomfortable and you're not safe. And our generation man with organized sports and play dates and all of that stuff, the baby boomer parents have just sheltered their kids like crazy to where true exploration is almost nonexistent. And so I think as a result, these kids, a lot of 'em are lost because they don't really know who they are. I've talked to so many of them who say, you know what passion, man, I saw that on Instagram the other day. Somebody talking about passion. I know I should have a passion. I just don't know what passion is. I don't feel it. I don't know what my passion is. So helping through fly fishing, through exploration, helping some of these young people really find themselves and find out who they are is a big part of what our higher calling is.", "token_estimate": 787, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "20:41", "end_timestamp": "20:41", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "746dd86e-6a79-4d51-b50b-2abb8719f280", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (24:48): I love that. How do you do that though?\n\nAl Perkinson (24:52): Well, you can support other folks who are doing it and then you can do things that sort of enable exploration or inspire exploration. We all make a lot of content, but content, I don't really like the word content. I like the word stories. And we know that story is so incredibly important to move people. No one has ever waged a war without a great story behind it. The soldiers are following the story. Religion is all built on story. Every great film is built on story. So to telling great stories that where the moral of your story is what the message you're trying to get across is, I think that is one great way just to tell inspiring stories. But we also work with other groups. There's a little group called Fish for Change, and a friend of ours, Steve Brown started it. Heather Harkavy is running it, and they do these summer camps where they bring high schoolers to Guha in the Bay Allens of Honduras or Costa Rica or The Bahamas, even Mexico and the Keys. And they spend a week teaching 'em how to fly fish. They teach 'em about conservation. They do all kinds of exercises to sort of challenge each other, and they come back transformed and changed for the better. (26:35): And so we're doing some interesting projects. One we're you're familiar with youth hostels and people travel and stay in youth hostels because it's a really inexpensive way to travel. Well, we're creating fishing hostels. So these are hostels where people can go and stay and fly fish, and it doesn't cost three or $4,000 a week like lodges do. It's 14 or 15 bucks a night. You've got the exclusive on this. We're just now kicking it off in the first part of October, but just starting to build the first one. But we plan to have these all over the world and to create a community of people who are traveling to fish and to explore and to find themselves.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:30): That sounds incredible.\n\nAl Perkinson (27:33): Well, good.", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "24:48", "end_timestamp": "27:33", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "2f134ef6-fe86-42cf-b3af-8da4bfa678b2", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:34): Yeah. Oh, man. Well, I'm excited to see that continue to roll out. One of the other points that you mentioned was this idea of performance around the product. Does it work? Does it perform above and beyond the customer's expectations? I guess it probably goes without saying, obviously the product needs to be of quality in order for these other points to even be valid.\n\nAl Perkinson (27:59): Yeah, absolutely. And over the years, as part of that contract with corporate America, and one of the ways that that contract has been broken over and over and over again, it used to be you go and buy a pound of coffee and I'm dating myself, and then all of a sudden they took a few ounces of it and it looked the same, but it wasn't a pound anymore. And they're like, gotcha. We saved some money. And you still think it's a pound of coffee, and it's a tiny example, but that happened over and over and over again. The whole discounting of America with the big, whether it's Sam's Club or Costco or Target or Kmart, what those consumers have said, look, man, getting back to the narrative of money and possessions or everything, they're like, I can't really have the good stuff. I at least want to look like I have the good stuff. And so they buy these lookalike products at discounts that are made all over the world through these crazy supply chains, a lot of it in Asia, but elsewhere too. (29:11): And so I think what's happening is that quality has been sucked out of so many of our products over time. And it's not just quality of the product, but it's quality of the service. I mean, customer service, I guarantee you, we all have 20 nightmare customer service stories that we could tell. And with all the merger and craze that has happened, lots of times the customer service department is the one that gets cut first, and it's kind of like, eh, consumers, if it breaks, they'll just have to buy another one. So I think there has been this decline in performance in the last, I don't know, 15 years maybe. There's been a whole nother class of products. It's made for professionals. Well, it used to be that all products were made for professionals, but now you have to spend a lot of money and quality is really the only people who can get it are the people who can afford a Viking stove or a Mercedes-Benz car or super high-end type of stuff. So anyway, I do believe that if you want to keep the faith with the consumer, if you want to keep that contract intact, if you really want to be authentic, then you have to be serious about performance quality. And there can't be an asterisk by it. (30:49): It's got to be performance and quality even when it hurts. So you screwed up and something went wrong, well step up, own up and fix it and pay for it. That's when if people are really committed to quality and really committed to performance, I think that's, you're right, it's central to everything else. Although if you have a strong brand, it can at least buy you some time and provide some equity that it takes to fix some of these other problems that you have.", "token_estimate": 773, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "27:34", "end_timestamp": "27:59", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "81acaf2d-b108-42e8-87b8-fa1420ade417", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (31:30): And from your guys' perspective, having quality product, does that just mean having a really strong quality control program or process, or how do you make sure that everything is up to code?\n\nAl Perkinson (31:43): Well, yeah, I mean, it starts in design and you design in quality in a lot of cases. So for example, if this is very specific to the sunglass world, but if your frame is made by one company in one country and your lens is made by another company in another country, then those two, when they get put together, they have to have the same curve. If they don't have the same curve, then one of them gets bent out of shape. If the lens is plastic, then it's probably going to be the one that gets bent out of shape. And what happens when it gets bent out of shape is that the view is distorted. If it's a glass lens, then the frame's probably going to get bent out of shape and it's not going to fit the way that it's supposed to fit. So coming up with design that sort of builds in and guarantees quality is the first step in the process. (32:51): Then there's manufacturing and all the things that you have to go through to create molds or to select materials that are high quality materials to find manufacturing partners. You're trying to hit price points. And so there are limitations. You can't just make the most expensive best of everything. There's a value equation that comes into play. And then once it all comes together and you put it in the market quality control, and we make the stuff here, cut the lenses, put 'em together. And so each piece has to be inspected by somebody who knows what they're doing. And even then they miss stuff. So once it gets out into the market, then you have to have a system that allows people to return things and you can service them really quickly. You don't charge them if it's your fault, you don't charge 'em anything. If it's their fault, you're reasonable on what those repair charges might be. So it really goes from design concept all the way through to customer service. And there's so many places where it can break down along the way that you really have to stay on top of it at all times.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "31:30", "end_timestamp": "31:43", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "66479810-01f8-451f-b58e-cf607ffed074", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:05): One of the points you mentioned, which ties into this is design and using design, I believe, as you said, as a weapon. Explain more. How do you use design as a weapon to creating an authentic brand?\n\nAl Perkinson (34:18): Well, I think you tie it into what your values are, and design is a visual way of sending a signal to people. I think one of my favorite brands in terms of design, but there's a bunch of 'em, but Arc TerraX is an outdoor brand, and man, you can spot an Arc Alteryx product from a mile away. We moved to Bozeman for a while, and so we went to the skis store to get some skis. We were going to be skiing a lot, and I was looking up at the wall of boots, the wall of ski boots, and I said, what is that? And he said, oh, that's the Arc Taric ski boot. I'm like, of course it is. I should have known that thing is unbelievable. And so before me as a consumer, before I even tried on and it was way out of my price range so I couldn't buy it, but before I even tried on, before I even learned more about it, the design of that product is sending me a message that this thing is simple, it's clean, it's high performance. (35:26): So it kind of sets the tone for that interaction. I think the Volkswagen Beetle was another incredible example. It was like a bug. It was super simple meant for the common person. So it really embodies all the values of that brand. So anyway, I do think it sets the tone. I look at other brands like Target is a great example of that. Their shelves and their stores are shorter than Walmart. And so that allows you to see over the top and give you a feeling of space that you don't get in a stack 'em high, cheap type of situation. They also bring in well-known designers to design their stuff. And so their whole thing is, Hey man, it's cheap chic and design doesn't have to cost a lot of money, and everybody deserves good design. And so that's a part of their whole corporate message of everybody deserves good stuff no matter what the price point might be. (36:39): So these design decisions that people make really say a lot about their overall product and service philosophy and their brand philosophy, and I just think they're incredibly important. Patagonia was also one early on that said, in all of our advertising that we do, all of our catalogs, the human is always going to be little compared to the environment. And that's because we believe that the environment is that we're just here for a short amount of time and that we're in service of the environment. And as you know, their whole corporate philosophy is use business to solve the environmental crisis. So those are just a few examples of design being a weapon.", "token_estimate": 676, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "34:05", "end_timestamp": "34:18", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "856af468-9da1-4b79-b53a-72929b5ad357", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (37:26): How do you use that weapon and your guys' own design?\n\nAl Perkinson (37:29): Well, I think we started with packaging and certainly everything that we do has an environmental or sustainability story to it. And we came out with a leather case and like a cow hide leather case, which most people would say is not sustainable. But then we looked at our head of product design is like a sustainability freak and knows he can get super deep, he could spend a whole hour with you just talking about our case. But when you break it all down in terms of, Hey, this is a food byproduct and it's tanned with water soluble tanning materials, so it's not harmful to the environment, hey, it never wears out. So instead of buying four cases in your life, you can buy one. So that cuts down on the material and the manufacturing that's made. And guess what? If you ever do throw it away, it's completely biodegradable. (38:33): So the only bad thing, the leather case was that one of the big things that's causing the rainforest to be destroyed in places like Brazil is they're creating new pasture land for cows. We love to eat meat so much from an environmental standpoint, meat is not good and we should all eat less of it and all eat more vegetables. So that was the one sticking point that we didn't like. So we kept looking and we found a vegan leather case that's made out of cactus, and we just introduced that probably a month ago. And cactus hardly uses any water to grow, as you might imagine. And then they tan it or dry it out in the sun, doesn't take any electricity, and then their manufacturing process is lead certified. So it hits all however many 750 different boxes that have to be checked, and it has a little bit of polyester in it, but that polyester is all made very sustainably. So it does leave a little residue when it biodegrades, but we compared the two and said, we're going to go switch over to cactus leather. And so again, it's designing into your product these attributes that say, this is something we care about and this is something that we believe in, and yes, it costs us more money, but it's worth it because it is good for the planet. So that's one of the things I think that we're doing.", "token_estimate": 560, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "37:26", "end_timestamp": "37:29", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "6249e619-1d3b-428c-a1d7-2db9fce281a1", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (40:19): I feel like I need to look up cactus leather now. I've never heard that. That's the thing.\n\nAl Perkinson (40:24): Yeah, there's all kinds of cool leathers out there now that are coming out. This one's made down in Mexico, and so our leather case was made over in Asia, so it also reduces the carbon footprint by bringing it up from Mexico instead of Asia, carbon neutral since inception. So that's real important to us. We measure that and we have to write a check if it's not. So this helped on that front too.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:51): And so kind of coming to our last point here, this idea of being true to your core, talk me through what does that mean in detail?", "token_estimate": 173, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "40:19", "end_timestamp": "40:51", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "7fc977c6-65f6-480f-9b31-e2cf7dbcc22c", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Al Perkinson (41:01): Well, part of it gets back to taking more of a human approach to marketing and to business than more of I guess a business approach. You hear a lot of people say, oh, it's just business. There's a different set of standards for business than for human beings when all businesses are a bunch of people. So it's a dodge and people hide behind this corporate shield. It's the same with the relationship you have with consumers. And we are seeing this real time because we're a brand new company. And so four months ago we had zero customers, zero. And so we have seen who has held up their hand and has come on board and saying, I believe what you believe. I love what you're doing. And they're friends and they're lifelong friends of our brand. And so to in the future, turn your back on those people because you want to chase bigger markets or you want to cut corners and grow more, I guess less expensively then, or to break the contract with those people in any way, whether it's through your higher calling or through customer service or anything else. (42:37): If you look at it from more of a human standpoint, it's just really doing your friend wrong or doing a member of your family wrong. And so we believe that instead of that, you have to listen to the core that these people came on and joined your brand, joined your tribe for a reason, and listen to what they have to say about where you're going to take the brand, listen to what they have to say about the practices that you're putting in place, the things that you're doing. Lot of times they hold up their hand and they're like, I don't agree with this. Well, instead of treating 'em like, oh, you don't know what you're talking about, you're just a consumer, you have to listen to 'em. And I think it's just smart to listen to 'em and they can just be a powerful force for your company and for your brand. (43:35): I believe that it's our job as marketers to build a tribe of followers and a tribe of believers. And that when you're marketing what you're going out and looking for, like Simon Sne says, you're looking for other people who believe what you believe. And if you can build a company and build a brand around a value system around shared beliefs around emotion, then you always have to do everything else right as well. But man, you've got people by the heart, you got 'em by the soul and they will fight for you. I know they will fight for you. I've seen it happen personally, and it's humbling to see. So respect your core sort of worship, your core value, your core. They're extremely important and they're the secret to you pulling in other people just like them. Super, super important.", "token_estimate": 673, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "41:01", "end_timestamp": "41:01", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "049757a2-83ac-48c2-bc07-e143e12c7dd3", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (44:39): So there's only five points. This all seems pretty easy.\n\nAl Perkinson (44:42): Yeah, well, I think it's easy to maybe understand when it's kind of difficult to execute, especially when some of these things don't sound very profitable. Truth be known, purpose-driven brands, authentic brands, tons of studies have been done are extremely more profitable than brands that are not. Consumers see it, they demand it, they understand it. But one of the biggest barriers to brand authenticity is ownership structure. It's funny because years ago, I think probably like 11 or 12 years ago, we started this little group called cem, SEAM, and was, I always forget what the acronym was, but it was basically for society, for the enhancement of authentic marketing. There you go. And so we invited heads of marketing for a lot of big brands to come together and go fly fishing out in Bozeman once a year for about a week and just talk about what problems each other was facing and building authentic brands. (45:59): And it was sort of like a self-help group. It was almost like Alcoholics Anonymous or something, and we all would tell our stories, and so many of them were the same. And one of the biggest ones was just around the barriers that ownership puts up. So if you're a publicly traded company, you have a fiduciary responsibility to return value to your shareholders each quarter, not over five years, but every quarter. And so a lot of shortcuts are taken that are really against product quality, against taking care of your core against a higher calling. And so I think that is just a huge barrier to being able to execute these things. (46:56): Private equity ownership is pretty much the same. They have a big fund. They've got to fiduciary responsibility to return a certain number to their shareholders. Really the only one that does not have those barriers is the independently held company. And so one reason I have an independently held company right now is that I have the freedom to execute these five things and to build an authentic brand. I just came to believe, and you could talk to Yvonne Ard about it at Patagonia, he owns that company and he can do what the hell he wants to do and he can take a longer view instead of a short-term view. And so again, I think that these things sound easy, but when you get into this, money means everything type of world that is out there and the focus on the short term, they become extremely difficult to actually execute.", "token_estimate": 626, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "44:39", "end_timestamp": "44:42", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "3e2fb562-7b5c-47a9-ac37-26a1e1e712c4", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (48:02): Yeah, I mean, well said. Much easier to talk about these things and actually walk the talk and make the decisions daily that reinforce these values.\n\nAl Perkinson (48:12): Yeah, for sure. There has to be somebody who is riding herd over this stuff. And even if you are, I'm glad we're doing this interview because this making me go back and reevaluate and make sure that I'm doing all this stuff so it's easy to slip away and not follow what you know you need to follow.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:36): If you had to add a sixth point to this list, what would you add? What didn't make this cut initially?", "token_estimate": 155, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "48:02", "end_timestamp": "48:36", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "b9f7fac2-d102-435c-beca-493f4d139724", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Al Perkinson (48:44): Wow, that's a good question, man. I'm not sure. There's a whole nother one that we didn't get to, which is around community, and so it comes out of committed to the core, but it's a whole nother way that the brand is like who you are as a person. But then when that person gets up and walks out into the world, how does that person behave and what does it do and how does it do it? So that's a whole nother section we call community-based marketing, and it's taking these authentic brand strategies and putting 'em to practice out in the world. So I think that is definitely one that's not the whole thing is there. And so we created this other strategy. The other thing I would say is the one thing that's not here is storytelling. And if you're going to be a really good marketer, we talked a little about it earlier, you really have to understand story and story. I'm a huge fan of Robert McKee and Robert McKee wrote a book called Story and he is sort of the story doctor for Hollywood. (50:08): When directors like John Cleese and I mean you name it, when they're having a problem with their story working, lots of times they'll go to Bob McKee and say, Bob, man, I need your help. Story doctorate. How do we fix this? But Bob, he's not like a technician. Bob a soulful dude, and he has these philosophies and deep hell beliefs on why story is so important to the health of our society and the quality of our lives. And he understands the power of story, and he's a student of story and he's gone through and he's sort of laid out what makes a story, how story works, and he does these seminars that are four days long, eight to 10 hours a day where that man at 75 gets up on stage with no notes and walks you through, here's how you tell a story. (51:10): It's an amazing experience and probably overkill for a marketer to sit through that, but the book is certainly great to read. But there's, you see a lot of these films out there in the outdoor industry these days, very few with any of 'em are done by people who know how to tell a story. They may have learned how to use a camera, and they may have some cool ideas, but very few of them have you sitting on the edge of your seat wanting to know how this is going to turn out and leaving the theater crying or laughing or wanting to do something that's going to make you a better person or change the world. True story does that, and if you can tell a real story with everything you do with your brand, you'll have a competitive advantage over 95% of the other brands out there, and you'll be able to move people to passion, move people to cult status, move people to care about you, to care what you believe in and to care about your brand.", "token_estimate": 681, "speakers": ["Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "48:44", "end_timestamp": "48:44", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "1c0de78a-e83e-4968-bfb4-f676912c3ad2", "episode_id": "8d824f33-ec9c-4fc5-83b0-9404ef8166cc", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4875", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (52:20): I mean, that's an entirely other podcast episode, and you're hitting home right there all about the essence and the art of storytelling. That's a complicated subject, but again, it's also not, it's very simple in theory, but practice, it's much more difficult. I feel like we need to have you back on for another two or three episodes in the future because we're running up against our end of our time for today, but I feel like there's so much that we could continue to explore.\n\nAl Perkinson (52:48): Love to man, anytime.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:51): Yeah. Let's see if we can make it happen in the meantime. Last question, what are you most excited for as you look to the future?\n\nAl Perkinson (52:59): I'm most excited about, honestly, the opportunity to pull more and more young people into this world of the outdoors that we're also passionate about and watch it change their lives. That's what it's all about. That's what our future is about, and if we don't do that, we're in a lot of trouble. If we do, we got a chance.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:25): Well said. If people want to follow along with you or with the company, where can they go to connect?\n\nAl Perkinson (53:30): The helio sunglasses.com. B-A-J-I-O is our website, and then that'll point 'em to our social pages and all that good stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:42): Awesome. Cool. Al, thank you for the time. I really appreciate it.\n\nAl Perkinson (53:46): Thank you, Cole. Enjoyed it, man.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:48): Yeah, likewise. I feel like I've got a few, I've been taking notes and I need to kind of sit on these points and digest them, so I appreciate it. This has been fun. And yeah, hopefully we'll see you again on the show shortly.\n\nAl Perkinson (54:01): Sounds great, man.\n\nCole Heilborn (54:02): Okay, take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Al Perkinson"], "start_timestamp": "52:20", "end_timestamp": "54:02", "podcast_index": 77, "episode_name": "EP 77: Al Perkinson | 5 Steps to Becoming an Authentic Brand | Bajio Sunglasses - Part 1", "slug": "ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-77-al-perkinson-5-steps-to-becoming-an-authentic-brand-bajio-sunglasses-part-1", "guest_name": "Al Perkinson", "guest_title": "Bajio Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "Al breaks down the 5 steps needed to create an authentic brand.", "podcast_length": "44:99"}, {"id": "a5a96aa0-6fae-48e1-85dd-ad3c704e82ba", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Ian Coble (00:03): It only serves their best interest. If you're new and you come in, you've only been shooting a couple of years and brands are approaching you and they're sliding that contract across the table that says Work for hire, and you're like, sure. You just left tons of money on the table, because they're not going to tell you that, oh, by the way, you could be making a lot more money here. They're not going to tell you that. If we don't educate that up and coming generation, they don't know and it's going to make it increasingly harder to charge for what an image is worth.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Back Country Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Heur. Today I'm sitting down with Ian Cowell. He's a photographer and director specializing in commercial work in the outdoor and adventure world. Ian, how are you doing?\n\nIan Coble (01:17): Doing great. How about yourself?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:19): Awesome. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nIan Coble (01:21): I am in West Seattle, so the beautiful island of West Seattle now that we no longer have a bridge.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:27): That's true. That's a pretty recent event I feel like in Seattle's history.\n\nIan Coble (01:32): It's been about a year now, a little over, and I mean, there are goods, there are bads with it, but when your office is in your house, it's not necessarily always a bad thing.", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Ian Coble", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:32", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "36751137-883b-427a-98e9-68ad85aed783", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:43): Fair enough. Well, I'm stoked to chat today. We're going to get down and gritty with the topic of licensing in the photography world. Could you provide a little bit of background on your career as a photographer? I know you've done a bunch of different work you've shot for brands like North Face, Brooks, Columbia, but kind of fill in some of the gaps.\n\nIan Coble (02:05): Yeah, I've been shooting professionally for 18 years. I went to Western Washington University up in Bellingham and got my undergrad degree in anthropology and finished that degree and then really had that, I think a lot of college educated can probably relate to it of like, well, what the hell am I going to do now? I've got this fancy piece of paper that I spent a lot of money on and I have no idea what I'm going to do. And I kicked around a couple of years and was working full time, just working in the restaurant industries and eventually kind of realized that all my free time was being spent outdoors, particularly skiing, and it was a thought of how can I make money at this? I'm already spending all my time here. This is where I want to be. If I can somehow create something where I can get paid to be here, then maybe that this is the best thing possible. (03:01): And this was before social media? This was before Instagram. This was back in analog days of film, not because film was hip, because film was the only option. And yeah, started the process of learning how to shoot photos and submit to magazines. And a lot of photographers, the editorial is the first place they kind of cut their teeth in the photography industry, and that's how I started. And then in time that led to advertising work for brands and the ski industry stuff like North Face or Heli Hansen or K two or Red Bull. And then I'd been doing nothing but ski work for probably I'd say eight to 10 years and just started segueing into more commercial jobs because the money was there. And B, it was a challenge. I felt like, not to say that shooting skiing isn't a challenge, it's an incredibly difficult thing. It's really tough to do, but I felt like I'd been doing it for 10 years and the challenge of shooting a power turn didn't feel hard anymore. And it was like, well, okay, how can I do something new where there's a challenge? And it was like, well, learning the world of advertising, photography and all the things that go into a large production shoot, that was something new to learn and that was really exciting. So I dunno, I'm probably rambling, but that's kind of a brief synopsis of where I came from.", "token_estimate": 651, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "01:43", "end_timestamp": "02:05", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "a7e007fd-c530-49cc-80d2-4dd05cf5bdbc", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (04:30): Okay, right on. So you were kind of right on the tail end of film world and then have been digital since, I'm assuming? Yeah.\n\nIan Coble (04:40): Yeah. I think about the first four years of my career were straight film. I still have binders in the closet behind me full of old slides that I can't make myself throw them away. I can't do it. It's going to break my heart if I have to do it. But yeah, I mean it was a great learning experience. At the same time you learn how light plays with film or how light interacts and it's something that you don't get quite as much until you really nerd out in digital, but to have to understand what exposure was in camera because if you didn't shoot it right, you didn't have anything that was usable because there was no Photoshop after the fact.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:26): Sure. Were you one of those photographers who used a light meter? Was that something that you practiced with?\n\nIan Coble (05:31): I did, yeah. I have some peers that still use light meters, and I actually really like that now to the point I've been doing this long enough, I can pretty much guesstimate with my eye and looking at the internal light meter in the camera. But I mean starting out, yeah, you almost had to have one or you had to really nerd out on what the light meter in your camera was possible. And I used to carry around a little notebook in my coat pocket, my breast pocket, and it would be like roll number three internal light meter said X shutter speed this, aperture that. And then I would compare it when I processed that role of film and it'd be like, oh wow, that was a stop and a half too dark. And it was like, okay, well here's how I have to adjust for the next one. There was a lot of this really weird nerding out, and it wasn't instant gratification. It was stuff where you had to really be fastidious and take good detailed notes because otherwise you get back a week, you get thrown back a week later from a trip or something, you'd be like, I have no idea what role was from, so I really have to start looking here.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "04:30", "end_timestamp": "05:31", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "aa0a1bc4-ec53-440b-ad31-c3c848f927ec", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:46): Yeah, cool. Well, some might say that those were the glory days for photographers. Things have certainly changed quite a bit between now and then technology wise, but also I think just the entire industry has changed and a piece of that is what we're talking about today. Licensing, I guess it's kind of an ambiguous topic. There's a lot of confusion I feel like around licensing. And I guess let's just dive in. So if you could just paint us a picture, why are we talking about this subject today? Why is it relevant? What are we trying to do I guess with this episode and from an educational perspective?", "token_estimate": 158, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:46", "end_timestamp": "06:46", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "5ab41a94-1bef-4b3c-9111-dd5d6a7d9ece", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Ian Coble (07:30): Yeah, I mean there's so many different ways we could kind of kick this conversation off, but I mean, I think maybe start off at the beginning in case I speak this jargon that's photographers, but maybe people don't know what licensing is. And licensing is essentially I take a photo or I shoot a video, and the company that hired me to do that has then purchases a license to utilize those for X amount of time for a certain territory, for a certain use. Maybe it's North American use of 10 images for 24 months from the date of first publication for website, social media and point of purchase. That's a license. And they can fluctuate hugely. They can go anywhere from like, we want to shoot an ad in National Geographic Magazine, it's going to run from October to November and that's it. Or it can be as sweeping as we want full rights globally in perpetuity forever. (08:42): We never want to have to talk to you again. And so there's this huge sliding scale that is what licensing is, and it is a really complicated thing. I think back in the day when I first started, the stock industry was sort of where licensing really became important. Stock photography is something like Getty Images, if you're familiar with that or iStock photo. But back in the day, Getty was the heavy hitter, and it still is to this day. If you look at any celebrity photo or Olympic photo, there's a Getty image stamped on it. They control that stuff. But the way that Getty made their money was if I'm a company and I need a photo of a businessman sitting at a conference table over his laptop and I don't have the time to hire a photographer to shoot it, I go to Getty, I click in what I want, and you give the exact usage, I mean down to the nitty gritty details, and they say that's going to be $1,500. (09:48): And that used to be a great resource as a photographer for understanding usages. I was a Getty photographer in my early days as well, so I had the portal to access it, but you could also sign up for it as any Josh Schmo off the street and just plug in the numbers and be like, oh, that's a $1,500 usage. Okay, that's good. I can go over to this client and be like, Hey, if you want to license this photo, it's going to be $1,500. And I know that's accurate because their computer told me that's accurate. (10:21): At the time, it was actually a really wonderful tool because you could say, and there were a lot of people who were like, well, this is different. This is, but it's just like, no, because at the same time, if you want to go buy that photo, that's the price you're going to go pay for it. I feel like, I hope I'm not getting ahead of myself now, but the industry has changed a lot with the advent of social media. Actually two things. One thing being social media, but the other thing was stock photography and it basically kind of shut down the big stock industry stock photography or stock video for that matter where it was rights managed, where every usage had a fee that you paid for it. And someone came along, and I can't remember what company it was, I want to say it was iStock photo, but it could have been something else, and basically said, Hey, for $10 you can use this photo for forever in as many ways as you want to. (11:20): And the business model was, we're going to make money off of volume, not off of quality. And I'm not saying that the photos that were on it were bad. I'm just saying their profit was doing volume, it wasn't doing single big sales. And when that hit, it really cut into the rights managed stock industry. I used to have a stock photography contract. I stopped doing it because it stopped selling when the royalty micro stock came out and you could get anything you wanted, you weren't seeing purchases anymore. People would rather take a lesser quality photo that they could buy for 10 bucks than spend 1500 or 2000 or $20,000 on a single photo. So I understand the business model, but it definitely cut under that value and that part of royalty free licensing then trickled into the commercial industry as well for photographers, for videographers, for anyone licensing content, because the expectation became we're not going to pay $1,500 for this anymore. (12:34): We are going to pay $10 for this or whatever that dollar is, and we don't want to be told how we can use it. We want to just own it outright, however the hell we want to use it. And that has significantly cut into photographers, videographers, creatives ability to license work because it's an uphill battle now of, well, why are we having to pay you this licensing fee, which shouldn't we just get it and pushing for more and more and more and more. You partner that with the advent of social media and how much content people need nowadays, how much brands need between different social media channels between their website, between e-commerce, between something like Amazon that needs its own unique assets or there is so many visual media needs that a company has now partnered, so their dollar has to be stretched a lot further than it used to be. If they had a million dollars to spend on photography in the past and it had to only produce four ads and a catalog, their budgets really haven't gone up in comparison to all the new uses. Now they still have the same million dollars, but they have to do all of those other things and stretch those dollars a lot thinner. So I don't know. I hope that sort of starts off that conversation of what licensing is, is why it's important and where it's going, at least", "token_estimate": 1389, "speakers": ["Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "07:30", "end_timestamp": "07:30", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "15b65866-d935-4b89-9834-523763cf2baa", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (14:12): For sure. How did licensing become a thing in the first place? So I guess maybe break down, how was the photographer typically paid back in the day and what role does licensing play in that?\n\nIan Coble (14:23): Yeah, and I do want to preface that this is me. I am not the Bible on licensing. I've been doing this for 18 years and I've got a lot of experience in it, but there are people that know a hell of a lot more than I do, and there are people that have been shooting for the same amount of time that I do or less, and they might even know more than I do, but this is just my 2 cents and the way I do business isn't the way that they do business. But here's my experience with it. And when I first started out and from understanding the people that I tutored under or interned under, cut my teeth learning editing for them or doing whatever they needed, you as a photographer would charge a day rate or a creative fee, and that was basically the cost of you showing up and taking photos. (15:15): And then as a line item underneath that would be something like your licensing fee. And in that licensing fee, it would be a separate fee from however much you charge for the day of. And that was relatively based on how much the usage was. If it was just for, I guess website really wasn't probably a thing, or if it was, it was very minimal because not a lot of people were using the internet 20 years ago. But if it was just for catalogs say that would maybe be a hell of a lot less than if it was print ads because that's where the huge license fees were coming in. (15:56): And the reason for licensing that first instance was you're getting paid to be creative. You're getting paid to shoot those photos. Then because of the value that that brand assigns to them based on the number of uses, you should in turn be compensated more for it. So again, if it only gets used one time, you deserve for easy math, you deserve a hundred dollars if it's going to get used one time, and that's by no means accurate, but if it's going to get used for 15 different uses over the course of 20 years, maybe you deserve $10,000. And so it was a way of compensating based on the value that the image you created had for that brand. So that's sort of the origins of licensing.", "token_estimate": 573, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "14:12", "end_timestamp": "14:23", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "887d1399-c267-4ec5-a9c4-b94fd1c3b218", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:47): Did licensing become a thing? Did photographers just, I guess how did that practice become a practice? Did photographers all just agree, Hey, we're just going to start providing a licensing line item in our budget as well?\n\nIan Coble (17:01): As long as I've been doing it, that was just the accepted practice. I don't know who got together however long ago commercial photography started and was like, Hey, this is what we're going to do. But as long as I've been aware of this as a profession, that was very much the standard practice that had been going on for decades.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:23): When was the instance in your career that you started to sense a shift in how things were being done? Was there a particular job?\n\nIan Coble (17:31): I don't think it was a particular job. I think it goes back to that shift from royalty or rights managed SOC to royalty free micro stock, and I can't think of a particular job, and it wasn't just Snap right there all of a sudden there, but it was sort of over the course of a couple of years and then all of a sudden you realize that you're getting pushback. A, my stock royalties went way the hell down, but B, every time I would negotiate a contract, the license ask became more and more and more without any expectation of having to increase the amount of money for it.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:14): There's a term out there called Work for Hire. What is Work for Hire? How does that work?\n\nIan Coble (18:20): Sure. Work for Hire is, it's been around as long as I've been shooting as well. This isn't a new term, but I think it's game in popularity. Work for Hire is where a company, a brand, whatever, hires a photographer, a videographer, it could be a graphic designer, it could be an artist, it could be anyone creating content. And the works that that artist creates under that contract become owned by the company that hired them. So the minute they create it, it is no longer theirs. It's now the ownership, the property of the company that contracted them.", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "16:47", "end_timestamp": "18:20", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "bbbae574-253b-4243-ae5d-8ba9752f169f", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:58): Okay, interesting. So when you're bidding on a job or you're negotiating contracts for a job now when brands approach you, is licensing even a thing that they're aware of or do they just expect everything to be worked for hire, or how much education or how much pushback do you have to do?\n\nIan Coble (19:21): It really depends. It's case specific. No, there isn't a single instance where it's uniform across the board. Traditionally, I would say newer companies that are like startups or you have someone that maybe isn't even in a startup, but they are new to the creative market, maybe they came in from finance and somehow managed to get into marketing, and then all of a sudden they're like, well, wait, why do we have to pay for rights? That's when you have to start doing the client education. But for brands that have been around for a really long time, they're big. They deal with photo shoots all the time or video shoots or whatever. They're very familiar with the idea of licensing. It doesn't mean they don't drive a hard bargain. A lot of 'em will try and push work for higher contracts because it's in their best interest to operate on that model. They own it. They don't have to worry about coming back to the photographer. But the bigger a company is, they definitely, they're familiar with licensing and the clients that I love are the ones where it's not a battle. They understand it and they expect it and they value your work and they want to compensate you in turn,\n\nCole Heilborn (20:45): I guess. But why does this, why is this important? Is it important?", "token_estimate": 401, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "18:58", "end_timestamp": "20:45", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}]
